// Generated by tools/gen-node-help.mjs from webtracker (src/graph/help.js, src/graph/hints.js). Do not edit by hand.

const NodeHelp kNodeHelp[] = {
  {"convolver", "Reverb from a recording: any sample in the project is the impulse response of a room, hall, plate, spring or speaker, and the sound is convolved with it. Mono or stereo responses work, up to 8 seconds.",
   {"Add a sample under the graph (Add sample), pick the response in the sample list, and keep mix near 0.2 to 0.4. The node does the rest, and the response is saved inside the project.", "Use predelay of 10 to 40 ms to keep drums clear of the tail, and lowcut around 150 to 300 Hz so the reverb does not muddy the bass.", "size changes the room: 0.5 is a small bright space, 2 is a cavern. decay fades a long response out sooner without editing the file.", "It is not only for rooms: the response of a speaker cabinet, a metal plate, a tin can or a spring gives a filter or a texture, and a short click-like sample gives a comb or a resonator.", "Switch the response from a pattern with the Smp column (expose sample on the node) for a different space in each part of the song."},
   {"A new response, size or decay is rebuilt in the background and takes a fraction of a second to arrive; the old one keeps playing until then.", "It costs more CPU than the algorithmic reverbs, and more with a long response. Trim a long file to what you need.", "The sound is about 3 ms late; the dry sound is not delayed to match. At mix 1 this does not matter.", "The response is read as it is (not shortened by silence at the start), and it is evened out to a fixed energy, so a very quiet or empty file gives no reverb."},
   {{"OpenAIR", "https://www.openairlib.net/", "real acoustic spaces: halls, churches, tunnels, stairwells; check the licence of each"}, {"EchoThief", "http://www.echothief.com/", "a large collection of recorded spaces"}, {"Voxengo", "https://www.voxengo.com/impulses/", "free reverb impulse responses"}, {"Airwindows", "http://www.airwindows.com/airwindows-impulses/", "large sampled spaces"}, {"Samplicity", "https://www.samplicity.com/download/", "free halls (and Bricasti M7 responses at another page on the site)"}, {"Balance Mastering", "http://www.balancemastering.com/free-funktion-one-impulse-responses-ir-irs-convolution/", "a club sound system and a former listening tower"}, {"Reason Studios list", "https://www.reasonstudios.com/blog/free-reverb-resources-where-to-find-impulse-responses", "a longer list, including guitar cabinets and classic reverb units"}},
   {}},
  {"voices", "Everything before a poly is done per voice, anything after a poly is done on the summed signal.",
   {"Put what makes the sound (oscillators, filters, envelopes) before it and the shared effects (reverb, delay, chorus) after it: they run once, not once per voice, which is also much cheaper."},
   {"A node after it hears the mixed sound, so it cannot treat one note differently from another: a per-note effect, such as Note pan, has to sit before it."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"notepan", "Gives every note its own place in the stereo field, chosen from the notes being played, and can swing or circle them round the listener. The place round the head has a front and a back, so a note that goes behind you gets quieter and duller.",
   {"Put it inside the voices (before the voices node): each sounding note is then its own copy and is placed on its own. After the voices it would pan the mixed sound as one.", "keyboard puts low notes left and high notes right, like a piano heard from the audience. fan spreads the notes you are holding evenly, in the order you played them, so a chord opens out. alternate throws notes left, right, left: good for arpeggios and fast lines.", "cluster places a note by how far it is from the average of what is held, so a chord is a bunch around the centre and a note far from it stands out to one side. mirror sets the notes in pairs either side of the centre.", "range is how much of the field is used: a little for a natural width, all of it for an obvious spread. centre moves the whole field.", "rate is real time in Hz (not tied to the tempo): 0.1 to 0.5 is a slow drift, 2 to 4 is a fast flutter. A negative rate turns the other way. swing goes back and forth by amp, triangle the same with straight sweeps, orbit goes all the way round and passes behind you.", "spread starts each note's turn a little after the last. With a held chord, the notes open and close like a fan instead of moving as a block.", "Drag across the circle on the panel to move the centre; the dots show where each sounding note is right now."},
   {"A turn needs a stereo output: on one speaker, or in mono, the movement is only a change in level.", "Pitch only decides the place in keyboard style (and mirror, cluster and random use it too); with alternate and fan it is the order of playing that counts.", "It places each note, not each frequency: a single note with many overtones moves as one.", "The back of the circle is a gentle level dip and low-pass, not a full binaural effect. For that use Head pan."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"reflect", "The first echoes of a room you set, made from the room's box: width, depth, height and how much the walls take. A plan shows the sound and you; drag them about and the echoes follow.",
   {"Put it before a reverb. The early echoes give the sense of a space and the reverb gives the tail, and the pair costs far less than a big reverb alone.", "Small rooms (4 to 8 m) give a close, boxy sound; 20 to 40 m gives clear separate echoes. Move the sound close to a wall to hear it come back from that side.", "absorb is how padded the walls are. damp takes the top off the echoes, as walls do.", "mix 0.3 to 0.6 gives presence; 1 and more makes a hall of mirrors."},
   {"It makes the first three bounces only, up to 32 echoes, with no tail.", "Mono in (the sides are added), stereo out."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"headpan", "Places a sound anywhere round the head: the far ear hears it a fraction of a millisecond later, quieter and duller, and a sound from behind loses its top. Mono in, a pair of ears out.",
   {"Best on headphones.", "Drag the dot round the head: round sets the angle, in and out the distance. At 90 degrees the time difference is about 0.66 ms.", "Sweep angle from a pattern for a sound that moves round the listener."},
   {"On loudspeakers the effect is much weaker.", "It models a head, not an ear shape, so up and down are not changed."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"lone", "A lead synth in one node: saw, pulse with pulse-width modulation, a sub oscillator and noise, with the Braids wavetable beside them, then hard sync, a wave folder and a ladder low-pass, with envelopes for the filter, the amplitude and the wavetable position, and an LFO that fades in.",
   {"One voice (the machine's default) is a mono lead: set glide or slide and legato for runs that stay connected.", "Put the wavetable level up and the saw down, then give position an envelope amount for a note that opens up as it plays.", "Raise Voices on the machine for chords; each voice has its own copy of everything.", "Write position, pulse width, sync ratio and fold from the pattern to move them note by note."},
   {"Glide and legato belong to one voice: with several voices a new note takes a free voice, so there is little to glide from.", "Hard sync and the folder are not band-limited, and bright settings alias a little."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"khufu", "The voice of a string machine: three sawtooth registers an octave apart, cut from one phase so they never drift, through a low cut and a gentle low-pass, with a drawn amplitude envelope. On its own it is plain; with an ensemble after the voices it is a string section.",
   {"Play chords, wide ones: the instrument is made for held harmony, not for lines.", "high up and low down for the classic thin, glassy strings; low up for cellos under a pad.", "Draw a slow rise and a long release for strings, or a sharp start and no sustain for a plucked, harpsichord-like tone.", "Keep it in front of voices and put the ensemble after them, so one ensemble works on the whole chord. A phaser after that is the other half of the classic sound.", "Raise lowcut when there is a bass in the song."},
   {"Monophonic: the last note wins (put it in front of voices for chords).", "The Khufu machine starts with 8 voices; raise Voices for bigger chords with long releases.", "The picture shows the registers as they are mixed, before the low cut and the low-pass."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"ensemble", "The chorus of a string machine: three short delays, each moved by a slow and a fast wobble at once, each a third of a turn behind the last. It turns one plain wave into a section of players, and works on anything.",
   {"After the voices of a Khufu, mix at 1: that is the string machine.", "On an electric piano, a pad or a plain saw lead, with mix around 0.5, as a rich chorus.", "Lower shimmer for a calm, wide chorus; raise it for the nervous shimmer of the old machines.", "Two ensembles in a row at different speeds are thicker still."},
   {"The sound is mixed to mono on the way in; the width comes from the three delays.", "At mix 1 the sound is about 6 ms late."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"rotary", "A rotating speaker cabinet: the top of the sound goes to a spinning horn and the bottom to a drum spinning the other way, heard by two microphones. The pitch, the loudness and the tone all wobble with each turn, differently in each ear. The switch between slow and fast is not instant: the rotors glide, and that glide is the thing to play.",
   {"On an organ-like sound: slow for chords, a switch to fast as a phrase rises, back to slow as it ends. Write speed in the pattern.", "On pads, strings and electric pianos slow is a gentle, wide movement that never repeats exactly, since the two rotors turn at different speeds.", "Stop leaves the cabinet's tone and drive without the movement.", "Raise drive for the growl of a cabinet played loud; it comes before the split, so chords thicken.", "Lower inertia for a quicker, more modern switch; raise it for long swells."},
   {"The sound is mixed to mono before the cabinet; the width comes from the two microphones.", "A picture of the cabinet, not a measurement of one: no cabinet resonances and no room.", "The panel shows the rotors turning by the same rule as the sound, but it is not read from the sound: after a pattern has written speed while the panel was closed the two can be a turn apart."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"haas", "One side is played a few milliseconds late, which the ear takes as one wider sound, coming from the early side.",
   {"6 to 25 ms is the useful range. Past 35 ms it is heard as an echo.", "Damp the late side a little: a duller copy sounds more natural.", "The panel shows the comb of notches the two sides make in mono; keep time short or mix low for material that will be played in mono."},
   {"The sound seems to come from the early side, so it is a widener that leans.", "It is not mono-safe at full mix."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"ringmod", "A ring modulator whose carrier can follow the pitch of the sound, so the sidebands stay tuned to the note: a melody stays a melody while the sound turns metallic or bell-like.",
   {"Track on, ratio 1 doubles the overtones; 0.5 and 2 give octaves; 1.5 a fifth. Add a few Hz of offset for a bell.", "Track off is the classic ring modulator with a fixed carrier: low carriers (20 to 100 Hz) give a growl, high ones a metallic shimmer.", "depth under 1 blends the carrier in as a tremolo."},
   {"The pitch is found by counting zero crossings: good for a single voice, poor on chords and noise (it falls back to freq).", "The carrier slides to a new pitch over a few milliseconds."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"compass", "A vector synth with wave sequencing. Four lanes, A B C and D, sit at the points of a diamond, and a position on the diamond says how much of each is heard. A path you draw moves that position after each note starts. Each lane is a row of steps: a wave from a bank of 256, held for a number of song ticks and crossfaded into the next, so a lane can be one plain wave or a rhythm of changing timbres locked to the song.",
   {"Start with one step per lane and four different waves, and draw the path: that is the classic slow morph between four sounds.", "Add steps to one lane only, with short fades, for a rhythm that comes and goes as the path passes that corner.", "Lanes of different lengths drift against each other: 3, 4 and 5 ticks take 60 ticks to line up again.", "A step can be transposed: an octave or a fifth on a few steps turns a lane into a small arpeggio.", "Write x and y from the pattern to play the mix by hand; set path to 0 to switch the drawn path off.", "A few cents of detune and some width make the four lanes one wide sound."},
   {"Monophonic: the last note wins (put it in front of voices for chords).", "The waves are the 256 of the shared bank; your own waves cannot be loaded yet.", "The steps are timed in song ticks, so the sequences change speed with the tempo.", "The mix moves once per block of samples and slides in between, which is smooth enough for a path or an LFO but not for audio-rate modulation of x and y."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"waveseq", "One wave sequence on its own: a row of steps, each a wave from the bank held for a number of song ticks and crossfaded into the next. It is one of the four sources inside the compass synth.",
   {"Use four of them into a vecmix for a vector synth of your own, with a filter or an effect on each source.", "With a single step it is a clean oscillator with 256 waves to pick from.", "Give it an envelope and a gain after it: on its own it sounds until the note is cut."},
   {"Silent until the first note, like osc.", "Each note starts the sequence again from its first step."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"vecmix", "Mixes four sounds by one position on a diamond: a at the left point, b at the top, c at the right, d at the bottom. In the middle a quarter of each is heard; the four shares always add up to 1. It is the mixer inside the compass synth.",
   {"Wire a vecpath into x and y for a drawn movement, or two LFOs at different speeds for one that never repeats.", "It mixes anything: four samplers, four filters fed by the same sound, four effect sends."},
   {"Mono: a stereo sound wired in is mixed down.", "The position is read once per block of samples and the mix slides to it."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"vecpath", "A control source that travels a drawn path across a diamond after each note starts, and puts out where it is as x and y. It is the path inside the compass synth.",
   {"Wire x and y into a vecmix.", "Use only one of the outputs as a drawn, multi-stage modulation for a cutoff or a pitch."},
   {"x and y run from -1 to 1; a parameter port wants 0 to 1, so put a range node in between for those."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"pulsar", "Pulsar synthesis: a short burst of waveform, the pulsaret, fired over and over. The note sets how often it fires and the formant sets how fast the burst plays, so pitch and colour are two separate controls. A drawn window shapes each burst.",
   {"Raise the formant against a fixed note for a vowel-like sweep; draw the sweep so each note opens and settles.", "A formant below the pitch leaves silence between bursts (a clicky, rhythmic tone); above it the bursts fill the period (a thick, formant-rich tone).", "mask and jitter break the train up into glitches; scatter spreads the bursts across the stereo field.", "Draw a sharp-cornered window for a harsher sound, a soft hump for a smooth one."},
   {"Monophonic: the last note wins (put it in front of voices for chords).", "Saw, square and noise bursts are not band-limited, so they alias at high formants."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"pulsetrain", "The pulsar oscillator on its own, for building patches by hand: feed its fm and form inputs from envelopes or LFOs, or run it through a filter.",
   {"Sweep the form input with an LFO for moving vowels.", "Use it as a modulator: low pitch, high formant, with mask for rhythmic gating."},
   {"Silent until the first note, like osc."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"sympathy", "A set of tuned strings that ring when the sound has a partial of their pitch, like the undamped strings of a piano or a sitar. Choose a chord, or let the strings follow the notes being played.",
   {"Chord mode: pick a root and chord, then play things in that key; the strings answer.", "follow notes: the strings are tuned to what the instrument plays (the note, its octave, its twelfth ...), so it works with any melody. Put it after the voices.", "ringtime 2 to 4 s is a piano; 8 s is a drone. damp makes the ring warmer. A little detune makes it shimmer.", "mix 0.3 to 0.6 and it is a halo; higher and the strings become the sound."},
   {"It adds sound only where the input has a partial near a string; a pure tone far from any string does nothing.", "At most 12 strings."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"noise", "Noise in every colour, from deep brown through pink and white to thin violet, set with tilt (the slope of the spectrum in dB per octave).",
   {"Hats and snares: white or blue noise through a highpass and a short envelope. Hi-hat air is mostly blue and violet.", "Pink noise sounds even to the ear and makes rain, surf and breath; brown makes wind, thunder and rumble under a kick.", "Sweep tilt with a pattern column or an envelope for a noise that darkens or brightens during the hit.", "Free on gives a constant bed. Put it through a filter and an envelope follower or a gate, so it only speaks when you want.", "Restart on makes every hit identical (a snare that never changes); turn it off and each hit differs, which is better for open hats and washes."},
   {"Brown noise carries a lot of energy at the lowest frequencies: put a highpass after it or it will eat your headroom and your speakers.", "The slopes are approximations of the ideal colours, and exact only in the middle of the audio range.", "A free-running noise never lets the instrument go quiet, so it keeps using CPU."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"specpan", "Splits the sound into ten bands, from 150 Hz to 10 kHz, and gives each band its own place in the stereo field. Below the low mono frequency the sound is made mono. Used for widening and for keeping the low end in the middle.",
   {"Put it on a pad, a bus or the whole mix, and start with spread around 0.2 to 0.4. It widens without delays or chorus, so the sound stays tight.", "Keep low mono at 80 to 150 Hz. Kick and bass stay in the centre and the mix holds up on club systems and in mono.", "shape 0 (alternate) is the airy widener, 1 (sweep) leans the highs to one side, 2 (wave) is two smooth swings. 3 (custom) uses the sliders b1 (lowest band) to b10 (highest).", "Write rotate from a pattern, or drive it from a slow lfo, and the image drifts along the spectrum.", "Use mix to blend the effect in: at 0.5 it is half as wide."},
   {"A band moves toward the pan position by blending, so the pattern is smooth rather than sharp, and bands bleed into each other near the crossovers.", "Widening by band thins the sound when it is summed to mono. Check in mono, and lower spread if the mids go hollow.", "The bands are fixed. The crossover points cannot be moved, only the low mono frequency.", "It works on the whole sound: it cannot pan one instrument inside a mixed signal.", "Do not put it on a kick or bass sound. Width belongs to the mids and the highs."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"compressor", "Turns the loud parts down so the level evens out; the make-up gain then brings the whole sound back up.",
   {"Slow attack lets the front of a drum through, so it keeps its punch; a fast attack calms it.", "Release should breathe with the music: long enough not to pump, short enough to recover before the next hit.", "A sidechain from the kick (the sidechain node) makes the bass and pads duck under it."},
   {"It cannot fix a sound that is wrong to begin with, and heavy settings make quiet details louder with the noise."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"hall", "A reverb: the sound is placed in a large room. The tail is what is heard after the sound stops.",
   {"Use it on a send or on one instrument, not on everything: a reverb on all of a mix turns it to mud.", "Short pre-delay keeps the dry sound clear in front of the tail.", "Turn the damping up to keep the tail dark so it stays out of the way of the hats."},
   {"A long tail runs on for a while after the last note, and costs CPU while it rings."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"delay", "Repeats the sound after a time. With feedback, the repeats repeat.",
   {"Set the time from the tick lines on the slider: the repeats then fall exactly on the grid.", "Low-pass the feedback path (or keep it low) so each repeat is duller and sits behind the last.", "Feedback close to 1 rings on for a long time; give yourself a way to turn it down."},
   {"Moving the time while the sound plays bends the pitch of the repeats."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"filter", "Removes part of the spectrum: a lowpass takes the highs, a highpass the lows, a bandpass keeps a band.",
   {"Sweep the cutoff from a pattern column or an envelope: that is most of the sound of electronic music.", "Some resonance gives the sweep a voice; a lot of it whistles."},
   {"A resonant filter can be much louder than its input at the cutoff: watch the level."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"freqshift", "Moves every frequency by a fixed number of Hz. Unlike a pitch shift, the harmonics no longer line up, so the sound turns metallic.",
   {"A few Hz of shift gives a slow, shimmering detune; hundreds of Hz gives bells and robot voices.", "Feedback makes spirals that climb or fall forever. Use small values and keep a hand on mix."},
   {"It does not keep musical intervals: a shifted chord is not a transposed chord."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"sampler", "Plays the samples of the instrument, one note at a time or in repeating bursts. Besides pitch and level it has a start position, slices, loops, a new-note action, and a retrigger engine that can repeat, move through the sample, change pitch and speed, and scatter the repeats randomly into grains.",
   {"One-shots (drums): leave grain and the jitters at 0. Each hit plays to its end and rings over the next note.", "Stutters and rolls: put an interval in the Rtg column (1000 is one tick, 0800 half a tick) and the repeats follow until the note ends. 0A reverse and 11 flip turn them backwards.", "Fake granular synthesis: a short Rtg, a grain length a little longer than the interval, a tiny 08 shift so the window creeps through the sample, and a little jitter on offset, pitch and pan. Sustained notes become clouds.", "Use a long, slow sample (a pad, a voice, a field recording), set the Off column to the interesting part, and let 08 shift walk away from it.", "Rising and falling rolls: 0D accel below 1 speeds the repeats up, above 1 slows them. 0E step makes each repeat a semitone (or any amount) higher.", "Everything is a pattern command, so a roll can change from row to row. Write 0000 to the command to turn it off again."},
   {"Sixteen voices per instrument. A big cloud (short interval, long grains) uses a voice per grain in flight, and the oldest is stolen when they run out: shorten the grain or slow the rate if it starts to sound thin.", "The jitters come from a random number generator that is not reset, so a bar with jitter sounds a little different each time it plays. Exported renders differ from run to run.", "Repeats keep going until the note ends: a new note, a note off or a cut. A repeat that would start past the end (or before the start) of the sample ends the chain.", "The gap between repeats cannot go below 1 ms, so a strong accel does not run away.", "No time stretching: grain length and pitch are independent only in the sense that you choose both. Every sound plays at the speed that its pitch implies."},
   {},
   {{"How a note plays", {"A note starts a sound at the start position (the Off column, or a slice marker if the 10 slice command points at one) and plays it forward, or backward when 0A reverse is 1. The sample's own loops apply: a looping sample plays on until the note ends, a one-shot plays to its end.", "A new note fades out what its column was still playing over nnaFade ms (new-note action), so a new hit cuts the one before it. Write 1F nnaAll 0000 to let one-shots ring on instead."}, {}},
     {"Making a new note cut the old one (or not)", {"By default a new note cuts the note its own column was still playing, over the 06 nnaFade time in ms (0000 cuts it at once, 0014 is the default 20 ms). Notes in other columns, and chords, are left alone.", "To let one-shots (drums, hits) ring on over the next note instead, write 1F nnaAll 0000 in a command column of the sampler's lane (on the first row is enough, it stays until changed); 1F 0001 turns the cutting back on. Looping samples always fade out."}, {}},
     {"Repeats: how a note becomes a chain", {"Give the note an interval in the Rtg column and it repeats. The interval is in ticks, written as four hex digits: 1000 is one tick, 0800 half a tick, 0400 a quarter, 2000 two ticks.", "07 repeats says how many (0 means until the note ends). Before each repeat the chain moves on: 08 shift moves the start along the sample, 09 ramp multiplies the volume, 0E step raises the pitch, 11 flip turns the direction round, and 0D accel changes the interval for the repeat after.", "Without a grain length, each repeat cuts the one before (2 ms crossfade), like a stutter. With a grain length the sounds end on their own and overlap."}, {}},
     {"Grains", {"0B grain is how long each sound lasts. Every sound, the first and each repeat, gets a fade in and a fade out; 0C edge sets how much of the grain is fade (0 hard edges of 2 ms, 1 a triangle, 8000-ish halfway).", "A grain a little longer than the interval overlaps the next one, which smooths into a continuous sound. A grain shorter than the interval leaves gaps and sounds like a gate. The sample pitch and the grain length are independent: shift moves through the sample slowly while grains keep their own pitch.", "The jitters make the texture. 13 jOffset moves each grain's start, 14 jPitch detunes it, 15 jPan places it, 16 jTime makes the repeat late, 17 jLevel makes it quieter. Each takes a maximum; the actual value is random between none and that.", "12 prob drops repeats at random (the timing carries on, so it is a gap, not a slowdown)."}, {}},
     {"Recipes", {"Cloud: Rtg 0400, 07 repeats 0000, 0B grain 1800, 0C edge 8000, 08 shift 8040, 13 jOffset 0200, 14 jPitch 0800, 15 jPan 4000. Hold the note and the cloud drifts through the sample.", "Rising roll: Rtg 1000, 07 repeats 0010, 0D accel 6000, 0E step 8800, 09 ramp B000. Sixteen hits, each faster, a semitone higher, and getting louder.", "Tape stop: 0F slide 7800 bends down one semitone every tick for as long as the sound plays.", "Gated chop: 0B grain 0400, Rtg 0800, 12 prob C000, 11 flip 0001. Quarter-tick chops, a quarter missing, every other one backwards.", "Fade out a held note: 1A 0C18 (down 24 dB over two ticks), or 19 0C01 for a fade to nearly nothing in two ticks. Write the slide on the row where the fade should start; the note keeps playing underneath. A slide stops at its target, so write another to go on.", "Reset: write 0000 to 0B grain, 08 shift 8000, 0D accel 8000, 0E step 8000, 0F slide 8000, 11 flip 0000, 12 prob FFFF and the jitters 0000. (Rtg 0000 stops the repeats.)"}, {}},
     {"Absolute offsets (FF, F0)", {"The Off column is four hex digits, so on its own it can only say where in the sample to start as a fraction, 0000 the start to FFFF the end: on a long sample each step is many frames. Command FF switches this instance of the sampler to absolute offsets: Off then holds a frame number.", "A frame number can be bigger than four digits, so it is written in two parts, hh:hhhh. The last four digits go in Off. The block count, how many full blocks of 65536 frames (0000 to FFFF) have gone by, is the value of FF. The Samples screen shows slice positions the same way.", "Example: Off 0300 and the command FF0002 start the note at frame 2:0300, which is 2 x 65536 + 768 = 131840. Off 0300 with FF0000 is frame 768. Frame 70000 is 1:1170, so FF0001 and Off 1170.", "It stays in force, for this instrument only, until you write F0 (any value; F00000 will do), which puts Off back to a fraction of the sample. A slice marker (command 10) still wins over Off, and reverse still counts from the end. A frame past the end of the sample starts at the end."}, {}},
     {"The command list", {}, {{"Smp", "column: which sample of the instrument's list plays"}, {"Off", "column: start position, 0000 the start, FFFF the end"}, {"Rtg", "column: ticks between repeats, 1000 is one tick, 0000 no repeats"}, {"01 / 02", "filter cutoff / resonance"}, {"03, 04, 05", "delay time / feedback / mix"}, {"06 nnaFade", "ms for the old sound to fade out when a new note starts in its column (looping samples always; one-shots when 1F nnaAll is 1, the default); 0000 cuts it at once"}, {"1F nnaAll", "0001 (the default): a new note cuts the one-shot its column was still playing (over the 06 nnaFade time), so hits choke each other; 0000: they ring on"}, {"07 repeats", "how many repeats (0000 until the note ends)"}, {"08 shift", "the start moves this far along the sample per repeat; 8000 none, above goes forward, below back"}, {"09 ramp", "each repeat's volume is multiplied by this; C000 is x1, 8000 halves it"}, {"0A reverse", "0001 plays backwards (the start counts from the end)"}, {"0B grain", "each sound lasts this many ticks (1000 is one tick); 0000 plays to the sample's end"}, {"0C edge", "fade share of a grain: 0000 hard, FFFF triangle"}, {"0D accel", "each repeat's interval is multiplied by this: 8000 is x1, 4000 halves it (speeds up), C000 is x1.5"}, {"0E step", "semitones each repeat is higher: 8000 none, 8800 +1, 7800 -1 (0800 is one semitone)"}, {"0F slide", "pitch bend while a sound plays, semitones per tick: 8000 none, 8800 up one per tick, 7800 down"}, {"10 slice", "start at a slice marker: 0000 the first, 0001 the second; FFFF puts Off back in charge"}, {"18 volLin", "XXYY: slide the volume in a straight line to the level YY/40 (hex; 40 is the sound's own level, 20 half, 80 double), in XX subticks (six to a tick; 00 at once). Written while a sound plays it slides that sound; before a note, every sound of the note"}, {"19 volLog", "XXYY: the same, even in dB (a steady fade); level 00 goes to about -80 dB"}, {"1A volDown", "XXYY: slide down by YY dB in XX subticks (1A 0606: 6 dB over one tick)"}, {"1B volUp", "XXYY: slide up by YY dB in XX subticks"}, {"1C / 1D", "XXYY: fine slide down / up by YY/256 dB (1C 0680: half a dB over one tick)"}, {"FF blockCount", "Off becomes an absolute frame number: the value is the block count, the full 65536-frame blocks before it, and Off the last four hex digits (FF0002 with Off 0300 starts at frame 2:0300)"}, {"F0", "back to relative: Off is a fraction of the sample again (any value, F00000 will do)"}, {"20 / 21", "XXYY: pitch slide down / up by XX semitones over YY subticks. Written while a sound plays it bends that sound; before a note, every sound of the note"}, {"22 / 23", "XXYY: the same in 256ths of a semitone (22 0680: half a semitone down over eight subticks)"}, {"24 portTo", "glide: a note that follows one still sounding in its column bends to the new pitch over XXY.Y subticks (0060: six subticks) instead of restarting. 0000 restarts as usual"}, {"25 / 26", "the shortest (XX.YY subticks) and longest (XXYY subticks) time between repeats; 0000 no limit"}, {"27 curve", "the nth interval is also multiplied by n to the power (value - 8000) / 4000: C000 lengthens them like n, 4000 shortens them like 1/n, 8000 is none"}, {"28 cut", "every sound is cut after XXX.Y ticks (0010 one tick, 0018 one and a half, 0100 sixteen); 0000 never"}, {"29 release", "every sound is released after XXX.Y ticks, and fades over the Release time (even a one-shot)"}, {"2A scale", "each repeat multiplies the cut and release times by this: 8000 is x1"}, {"2B / 2C / 2D", "stretch: the whole sample is spread over xxyy-8000 subticks (2B), ticks (2C), or ticks that hold when the repeat time changes (2D); each repeat starts as far along as the time since the last. Needs a repeat time (Rtg). 8000 off; negative goes backwards (set Off to FFFF)"}, {"60 to 67", "jump in the wave of the sounds playing, xxyy subticks (60 to 63) or ticks (64 to 67): 60 ahead, 61 back, 62 ahead and 63 back counted in the sound's playing speed; 64 to 67 the same in ticks"}, {"30 to 32", "a filter on each sound: 30 type (0 off, 1 low pass, 2 high pass, 3 and 4 band pass, 5 notch), 31 cutoff (0000 is 20 Hz, FFFF 20 kHz), 32 resonance"}, {"33 / 34 / 35", "cutoff slide down / up, XX semitones over YY subticks; 35 moves the cutoff that many semitones each repeat (8000 none, 0800 one semitone)"}, {"38 / 39", "humanize: a random cutoff up to XX.YY semitones, and each repeat late by up to XX.YY subticks"}, {"40 to 42", "vibrato: speed (the period in subticks, XXY.Y), depth (XX.YY semitones), wave (0 sine, 1 triangle, 2 square, 3 saw)"}, {"43 to 45", "cutoff LFO: speed, depth (semitones of cutoff), wave"}, {"50 to 53", "phases: 50 and 52 set the vibrato / cutoff LFO phase now, 51 and 53 make every note start at that phase (FFFF: free running)"}, {"A0 to AF", "reset to what the instrument starts with, additively: 1 the retrigger settings (shift, ramp, accel, step, flip, probability, limits, curve, cut scale, stretch), 2 humanization, 4 the filter, 8 vibrato and LFO. A0 and AF reset all"}, {"70 / 71", "save / load settings: XX which groups (added up as for Ax), YY one of 16 slots (00 to 0F), kept per lane. Handy for a texture you come back to"}, {"1E subdivide", "subticks in a tick, default 6: the unit of the slides' time (a slide of XX subticks lasts XX/subdivide ticks)"}, {"90 to 93", "the same as 1A to 1D, with XX in tens of milliseconds, whatever the tempo (90 0A06: 6 dB down over 100 ms)"}, {"11 flip", "0001: every repeat plays the other way round"}, {"12 prob", "chance a repeat sounds: FFFF always, 8000 half"}, {"13 jOffset", "random start, up to this fraction of the sample either way: FFFF is the whole sample"}, {"14 jPitch", "random pitch, up to this many semitones either way: FFFF is 12"}, {"15 jPan", "random pan: FFFF is anywhere from full left to full right"}, {"16 jTime", "each repeat late by a random part of the interval: FFFF up to a whole interval"}, {"17 jLevel", "random volume: FFFF down to silence at worst"}}}}},
  {"blur", "Smears the sound across frequency and time, so it melts into a breathy wash.",
   {"Use it on a send, with mix partly open, to turn a sound into a pad.", "Freeze holds the moment, so the sound hangs in the air until you release it."},
   {"It works in frames, so it adds delay (bigger size, more delay), and attacks are smeared whatever you do."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"hpss", "Splits a sound into its tones and its hits: harm gets the held notes, perc gets the drums and attacks. Wire both back together and you have the sound again, one frame late.",
   {"Put it on a full mix or a drum loop with a bassline, then process the two outputs differently: compress and crush perc, add reverb to harm.", "Turn the level of one output down to remove the drums from a loop, or the tones to leave only the hits.", "Length is how long a note must be held to count as a tone; width is how broad a click must be to count as a hit. Sharp makes the split harder."},
   {"It works in frames, so it adds delay (bigger size, more delay). Sounds that are both, such as a tuned kick, are shared out between the outputs."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"pogue", "A grubby multi-voice chorus. Up to eight voices read the sound back at delays that wander between mindelay and maxdelay; the voices are filtered, saturated and bit-reduced, and fed back. Clean it is a lush, drifting chorus; pushed, it smears and grinds.",
   {"For a wide, wobbly pad: 4 voices, delays 5 to 20 ms, change around 0.5, stereo wide.", "For a broken tape feel: resolution down to 30 to 60, inertia around 15 ms, a little degrade and saturation.", "Feedback with the low-pass down and some xfeedback gives a metallic, swirling ring; keep hpcut up so the bass stays clean."},
   {"The delay is at most 50 ms, so it choruses and flanges but does not echo. Strong feedback with saturation off can ring; saturation keeps it in check."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"bodyblur", "Smooths the body of a sound without touching its hits. The sound is split into held tones and attacks; the tones are blurred and rounded, the attacks pass straight through in time. A crushed pad turns silky while the snare keeps its snap.",
   {"Put it after a crusher or a gritty synth: smooth around 0.4, time 80 ms, and a little round, to take the crunch off the sustain while the hits stay sharp.", "Turn time up to 300 ms or more for a sound that blooms; turn smooth up for a wash.", "Use mix partly open to keep some of the original grit."},
   {"It works in frames, so it adds delay (bigger size, more delay). A sound that is both tone and hit, such as a tuned kick, is shared between body and hit, so only part of it is smoothed. Eco quality works out the split every other frame."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"islands", "The piano roll's \"add islands\", as an effect. In every frame the peak inside each loud blob of the spectrum is found and a thin ridge of sound around it is kept; the shoulders of the blobs, the smear between notes and the hiss are turned down. Pitched sound comes out cleaner, with each note standing alone.",
   {"Put it after a noisy recording of a chord or a melody: the notes stay, the hiss and the room fall away. Try contrast 0.5, islands 0.7, depth 40 dB.", "Raise depth and islands for a stark, resynthesized sound; lower them to just clean up.", "Smooth keeps the result from warbling. Raise it for pads and chords, lower it for fast melodies.", "Use size 8192 or 16384 to tell close low notes apart."},
   {"It works in long frames, so it adds a lot of delay (up to about a third of a second), and the attack of a drum blurs. Low smooth values or high islands can sound watery. It cannot hear a note that the noise has buried."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"specdyn", "A compressor for every frequency at once: loud frequencies are pressed back, quiet ones left alone, so booms and harsh rings are flattened without dulling the rest. Tilt then leans the whole spectrum brighter or darker.",
   {"Threshold -36 dB with ratio 4 evens out a full mix; lower the threshold and raise the ratio to flatten it further, and make up the gain.", "Tilt of +1 or +2 dB per octave makes a dull sound clear, -1 to -2 warms a harsh one.", "Smooth keeps the gain from changing too sharply from one frequency to the next, which stops a ringing, whistling sound."},
   {"It works in frames, so it adds delay (bigger size, more delay), and fast attacks cannot be faster than a frame."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"mosh", "A lost keyframe, as sound. The spectrum of the sound at in is held as a picture, and the movement of the sound at mod (louder, quieter, gliding up or down) is laid over it, so the old sound wears the new sound's motion. Every so often a keyframe puts the live sound back, and the smear starts to grow again.",
   {"Put a sustained pad or chord on in and a drum loop or a voice on mod: the pad pumps, slides and blooms to the other sound while keeping its own colour.", "Keyframe 1000 to 3000 ms with hold near 1 gives a clear cycle of clean, smear, snap back. 0 lets it smear for as long as the song runs.", "Raise bloom to grow ghost partials where the pad had nothing; keep it low for a pad that only bends. Heal draws the picture slowly back, so the smear is a stretch rather than a drift.", "Nothing on mod gives the freeze and stutter of a decoder that has lost its data: the picture holds until the next keyframe."},
   {"It works in frames, so it adds delay (bigger size, more delay). With nothing on in there is nothing to smear. The sideways motion is found in blocks, so a large glide is dragged in jumps, which is part of the sound."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"listen", "Plays the notes of another instrument. Put it in an instrument's graph (it has no wires), pick the instrument it listens to in its panel, and every note that instrument is given (patterns, Node Sequencer, keyboard, MIDI) plays here too, with its velocity, note off and cut. A quick way to hear how another machine would play the same notes, without touching the other lane or using replace.",
   {"Make a new instrument, put a listen node in its graph and choose the lane's instrument as the source. Every note column of that lane now plays both.", "Transpose lifts or drops what it hears (12 is an octave up), and level scales the velocity. Expose `on` as a column and a pattern can switch the listening on and off.", "Turn the source's Out `master` switch off to hear only the listener, or leave both to layer them.", "A ^^^ kill on the source kills the listener too."},
   {"It passes on notes (pitch, velocity, note off, cut), not the values of the source's other columns or its automation. Two instruments that listen to each other pass a note round once and stop."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"xsynth", "Cross synthesis: the shape of the sound at mod is laid over the sound at in. Put a voice on mod and a pad or noise on in, and the pad talks.",
   {"Use a sustained sound with a lot in it (noise, a saw pad, a chord) on in, and speech or a drum loop on mod.", "Smooth low keeps the modulator's fine detail (more like talking); high gives only its broad colour.", "Detail lends the modulator's harmonics too, so a sung note makes noise sing."},
   {"Where the modulator is silent the output is silent. It works in frames, so it adds delay (bigger size, more delay)."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"chord", "Turns every note into a chord you have typed as a name. Put it in an instrument's graph (it has no wires), the same way as the arp, and the notes the instrument is given (from patterns, the keyboard and MIDI) become chords before the voices hear them. The chords live in 256 slots, 00 to FF; the chord parameter (the Chd column in a pattern) says which slot plays, and the octave parameter (the Oct column) says where the root sits.",
   {"Type names into the slots in the node's panel: F# 13sus, Eb 9sus4, E min9(11), G Major 7th, Db 7#9(no5), Cm7/Bb. Each slot shows the notes it makes, and a name that cannot be read is marked red with the reason.", "By default the pattern note only triggers the chord: C-4 and F#-2 play the same notes, exactly as typed. Turn follow on and the chord moves with the note (C4 plays it as typed, D4 a tone higher), which gives you a chord keyboard.", "Set the chord in a row with the Chd column and the octave of its root with Oct (one hex digit, 2 to 9 is the useful range). Both stay until written again, so one pattern per chord, reused, is the tidy way to write a progression.", "A slot left empty plays the note as it is, so slot 00 can be left empty to switch chords off for a bar.", "Strum spreads the notes out in time, in ms between one note and the next; 20 to 40 ms is a guitar-like strum. Direction chooses whether it starts from the lowest note or the highest.", "Inversion lifts the lowest notes an octave, one at a time, and a slash chord (C/E) puts a bass note under the chord. Notes limits the size of the chord for a synth with few voices: the fifth is dropped first, then the highest notes.", "It works with any sound. To put a chord node in front of another instrument (Cardinal, for one), insert the Chord node into that instrument's graph, leave it unwired, set the voices node high enough for your biggest chord (7 for a 13th chord), and expose the chord and octave parameters as columns (width 2 hex for chord, 1 hex for octave).", "Every parameter can be written from a pattern column or driven by a wire, so the strum or the inversion can change during the song."},
   {"Only one node that takes notes is used per instrument (the first of the chord and the arp), so a chord cannot be fed into an arp yet.", "The notes it makes keep the velocity of the note that triggered the chord, scaled by level; there is no velocity per chord note.", "A note column plays one note at a time, but the chord notes are made after the column, so a chord of many notes needs enough voices (the voices node) or the extra notes take voices from each other.", "A strum is timed in audio blocks (about 3 ms), so a note may sit a few ms off its ideal place.", "Chord names are for tertian chords: stacked thirds with the usual alterations. Cluster chords or exact voicings are best written as notes in several note columns.", "The notes are limited to the 0 to 127 range: a chord that would go above it is moved down an octave as a whole."},
   {},
   {{"What can be typed", {"Spaces and brackets are free, so \"E min9 (11)\" is the same as \"Emin9(11)\". A name is a root, then pieces in any order; a slash at the end names a bass note."}, {{"Plain notes", "start with >, then the notes themselves: >G-4 A-5 C-5, >A2 C#3 E3, >57 60 64. They play at exactly those pitches."}, {"Root", "A to G, then # or b: C, F#, Eb, Bb. Lower case works too."}, {"Major", "nothing, or maj, Maj, Major, M, \316\224: C, Cmaj, CM"}, {"Minor", "m, min, minor, mi, -: Cm, Cmin, C-"}, {"Diminished", "dim, diminished, \302\260, o: Cdim, C\302\260""7 (the fully diminished seventh)"}, {"Half diminished", "\303\270, half-dim, or m7b5: C\303\270""7, Cm7b5"}, {"Augmented", "aug, augmented, +: Caug, C+7"}, {"Power chord", "5: C5 (root and fifth)"}, {"Sixth", "6, 6th, m6, 6/9 or 69 (sixth and ninth)"}, {"Seventh", "7, 7th: C7 (dominant), Cm7, Cmaj7, C\316\224""7, CmMaj7 (minor with a major seventh), Cdom7"}, {"Ninth, 11th, 13th", "9, 11, 13 (each includes the sevenths and the notes below it; 11th and 13th leave out what clashes): C9, Cm11, CMaj13"}, {"Suspended", "sus, sus4, sus2: they replace the third. C7sus4, F#13sus"}, {"Added notes", "add9, add11, add4, add2, or in brackets: Cmin9(11), C(add13)"}, {"Altered notes", "b5 #5 b9 #9 #11 b13, with or without brackets: C7#9, C7(#9#5), Cmaj7#11, C7b9"}, {"Altered dominant", "alt: C7alt (third, seventh, b9, #9, b13)"}, {"Left out", "no3, no5, omit5, in brackets or not: C7#9(no5), C9no3"}, {"Bass note (slash)", "a note below the chord: C/E, Am7/G, D/F#"}, {"Spacing", "spaces and brackets are free: \"E min9 (11)\" is \"Emin9(11)\""}}},
     {"How the notes are placed", {"The root sits in the octave of the octave parameter (4 is the octave of middle C) and the other notes are stacked above it, lowest to highest. The 9th, 11th and 13th are an octave up. A 13th chord leaves out the 11th, which fights the third, and a suspended chord leaves out the third (a suspended 4th chord also leaves out the 11th, since it is the same note). Name any note you want back with add, for example 13add11.", "A bass note (the part after a slash) goes below the lowest note, an octave lower if it would be only a second or less under it. Inversion lifts the lowest notes first, and the bass note is added after that."}, {}},
     {"In a pattern", {}, {{"Chd", "which slot plays, 2 hex digits: 00 to FF"}, {"Oct", "the octave of the root, 1 hex digit"}, {"Str", "the strum in ms, if it is exposed as a column (00 none, FF is 120 ms)"}, {"the note", "only triggers (unless follow is on); the note off releases the whole chord"}}}}},
  {"amp", "A valve guitar amplifier in one node: preamp, tone stack, power stage and speaker cabinet. It is a model of the way such an amp works (stages of valve-like gain that squash and round the signal, a passive three-band tone stack, a power stage that gives under load, a speaker that cuts everything above a few kHz), not a copy of one amp, and it makes no claim to any particular make. Put it after any sound; guitars, but also bass, synths, drums and vocals.",
   {"Start with gain 5, stages 2, master 4 and the cabinet on, then move gain: below 3 is clean, 4 to 6 is crunch, 7 and up is a lead sound. More stages make the same gain tighter and more compressed.", "The tone controls come after the preamp, as in a real amp, so they change the character of the distortion as well as the level: turning treble up does not just make it brighter, it makes the clipped top end buzzier. The three voicings move where the controls work.", "Bias is the valve's colour. 0 is symmetrical, harsh and fuzzy; 0.3 to 0.6 is warm, with even harmonics an octave above the notes, which is what makes a chord sound fuller.", "Sag is the feel of the amp. With sag up, loud chords squash for a moment and then bloom as they decay; with it at 0 the amp is stiff and tight, better for fast palm-muted playing. Sag matters most when master is high.", "Presence and depth are the output stage's top and bottom. Presence gives bite after heavy distortion; depth adds thump. They come after the power stage, before the speaker.", "The cabinet matters as much as the amp: without it the distortion sounds like a harsh buzz. Choose open 1x12 for loose and airy, closed 2x12 for tight, stack 4x12 for big and dark. If you want a real speaker, set cab to none and follow the amp with a Convolver loaded with a speaker impulse response.", "Use mix below 1 for a little of the dry sound back: the pick attack stays clear through heavy gain.", "It works on a synth too: a saw lead through gain 7, a cabinet and a little sag is a rock lead; a clean pad through gain 2 and the open cabinet is a gentle tube warmth."},
   {"It is a sound model, not a circuit simulation: it does not solve the circuit, so it has no interaction between the stages and the speaker, and no microphone placement.", "The cabinet is a few filters, not a recorded speaker. It gets the broad shape right (the low bump, the mid peak, the steep top) but not the detail. A Convolver with a real impulse response is better when it matters.", "It adds a little delay (a few samples, more with 4x oversampling). The dry signal for mix is delayed to match, but a parallel path in the graph is not.", "It is loud and changes loudness with every setting; set level last, and watch the output.", "High gain amplifies noise and any hum in the input. Use a gate node before the amp for a quiet sound, the way a noise gate sits before a guitar amp.", "With gain at 10 and four stages the result is extremely compressed: the dynamics of the playing are mostly gone."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"saturator", "Warmth and thickness from a curve, simply: push the sound into it and it rounds off, adding harmonics. The loudness is kept about the same, so you hear what the curve does rather than just a louder sound.",
   {"Drive 6 to 12 dB with soft or tape is a gentle glue for a drum bus or a mix; 20 dB and above is real grit.", "Tube and bias add even harmonics, which sound warmer and thicker than the odd ones of soft and clip.", "Raise bass to keep a kick or a bass clean while the rest saturates; 20 Hz leaves it off.", "Tone takes the top off after the curve; mix keeps some of the clean sound for parallel saturation."},
   {"Quality standard runs the curve at 2x oversampling to keep aliasing down; eco runs it at 1x and is cheaper, hq runs it at 4x. The sound is delayed by a few samples (the dry sound is matched)."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"mbsat", "The saturator in four bands, each with its own drive and gain, so the lows can warm gently while the highs get a bite, without the lows turning the highs to mush.",
   {"Start with the defaults (gentler on the lows) on a full mix or a drum bus, and turn the highs up for air and bite.", "Solo a band to hear what it adds; the bands add back to the original, so with every drive at 0 and gain at 0 the sound is nearly unchanged.", "Type, bias and quality are shared by all four bands; the crossovers work like the split node."},
   {"Four curves run at once, so it costs about four saturators; the quality switch (eco is 1x, standard 2x, hq 4x) is the way to save CPU."},
   {},
   {}},
  {"genexpr", "A node whose sound is code: a program in a small language modelled on GenExpr (the code part of Max's gen~), compiled once when you stop typing and run once per sample. It can be an effect (the sound arrives at in1 and in2) or an instrument (the notes arrive as freq, gate, vel, note and trig), and it can be both. There is no preset: the Templates list above the code box loads working examples to start from (sine voice, detuned pad, FM bell, plucked string, kick, hi-hat, echo, bitcrusher, ring modulator, soft clipper).",
   {"To play it from a pattern or the keyboard, put the node in front of a Voices node (code.out1 into the Voices node, then the out node). Every voice is its own copy of the code, with its own note, its own History values and its own delay lines, so chords and overlapping notes just work. The Code instrument in the new-instrument list is exactly this, starting from the sine voice.", "Draw a Param for everything you would want to move: it becomes a knob (p1 to p8, in the order declared) that you can expose as a pattern column, write with a CC command, wire to an LFO or move by hand. The knobs show 0 to 1 on the node and are stretched to the min and max you gave the Param.", "trig is 1 for the first sample after a note starts. Use it to restart an envelope (env = trig ? 1 : env * 0.999), to start a burst of noise for a plucked string, or to fire a drum. gate is 1 while the key is held: feed it to slide() for an attack and release envelope.", "Put a Chord node in front of the instrument (in the same graph, unwired) and every key plays a chord, each note of it going to a voice of your code.", "The velocity of the note is vel, 0 to 1: multiply it into the output, or into an FM index or a filter cutoff, for notes that get brighter when they are played harder.", "For glide between notes, smooth the pitch: f = slide(freq, 3000, 3000); and use f instead of freq (the numbers are in samples, so 3000 is about 60 ms at 48 kHz).", "Write a filter as a History and a line of arithmetic: lp = lp + (x - lp) * k; is a one-pole lowpass (k near 0 is dark, near 1 is open); two in a row are 12 dB per octave. A first-difference, x - previous x, is a crude highpass. The templates use both.", "A feedback delay line is a Delay: line.write(x + line.read(time) * feedback). With the time set to one period of the note (samplerate / freq, in samples) and a lowpass in the loop it is a plucked string; with a longer time it is an echo.", "Put the code in front of a Voices node for instruments, behind it (or in an instrument with no Voices node) for effects. As an effect, in1 and in2 are the left and right of the sound arriving, and the code sees both at once.", "Keep programs short and readable: a comment per line of what it is for (// ...). The code is saved inside the project with the node, and a template can be undone with the last item of the list (the code you had before).", "Watch the status line under the code: it says compiled, or how many mistakes. Mistakes are underlined where they are and listed with the line, the column and a hint; the last working code keeps playing while you fix them."},
   {"Notes are heard at the start of an audio block (about 3 ms), not sample by sample: freq, gate, vel and note hold still for the whole block, and trig is 1 for its first sample. A note can start up to a block late, and a change in pitch or gate is a step. Smooth them with slide() where it matters.", "freq is not per-sample pitch: there is no pitch bend or vibrato input, and no key aftertouch, mod wheel or CC. Make vibrato yourself (freq * (1 + 0.005 * cycle(5))) and write the other controls from pattern columns through the Param knobs.", "The output is not limited. Nothing stops out1 from being 10, which is very loud; keep the signal within -1 and 1 (tanh() or clamp() at the end if in doubt) and set the instrument's out gain. A result that is not a finite number is made 0, so a mistake in a calculation is silence, not a crash.", "An instrument made of this node needs its sound to go to 0 after the key is let go, or the voice never finishes: a voice is freed when it has been released and its output has been silent (under 0.00001) for three blocks. A drone, a DC offset or an envelope that does not use gate keeps the voice alive until it is stolen by another note.", "A voice that is played again keeps its History and Delay values from the last time (envelopes and strings carry on from where they were). Use trig to reset what has to start clean. A cut (the cut command, Panic) starts the code again with everything at 0, and so does every edit of the code that compiles.", "Out1 and out2 are two mono outputs. The Voices node takes a stereo pair: the Code instrument wires out1 to it, which is heard in both ears. There is no stereo image from a single code node; make a wide sound with two nodes (one for each side) if you need it, or put a chorus or a stereo effect after the Voices node.", "Only 2 inputs, 2 outputs and 8 Param knobs. There are no loops, no functions you write yourself and no arrays; the language is one pass of arithmetic per sample, with History for memory, Data and Delay for buffers and if / else for choices. A buffer is at most 4194304 samples (1.5 minutes at 48 kHz) and costs 4 bytes a sample for every voice.", "The sample rate, the tempo and the song position are not inputs: you have samplerate as a constant, but nothing to say where the beat is. Send timing in yourself: a pattern column on a Param knob, or an LFO wired to the knob.", "It cannot load a sample into a Data buffer; a Data starts at 0 and the code has to fill it (a wavetable written with sin() on the first samples, say). For playing a sample use the sampler.", "It is a program run by the browser's JavaScript engine, so it is fast but not free: the cost is per voice, per sample and per operation. Six voices of a few dozen operations is nothing; thirty voices of a delay-line reverb with a hundred taps is not.", "Naive oscillators alias: phasor() into a saw or a square, and a hard-driven tanh, have harmonics above half the sample rate that fold back as inharmonic whistles. It is a sound to write around (lowpass after, or add the harmonics up to a limit with sin()), not something it removes for you.", "The language is a subset of gen~'s code language, written to read the same, not a copy: names such as History, Param, Data, Delay, phasor, cycle, mix, scale, wrap, fold, mtof, dbtoa, slide, delta, change and dcblock are there; the rest of the gen~ library (filters, interpolators, MIDI, sample reading) is not. freq, gate, vel, note and trig are names of this node, so they cannot be used as a variable, a Param or a History name."},
   {},
   {{"The notes: freq, gate, vel, note, trig", {"These five names are always there to read. They cannot be set. They come from the notes played on the instrument, the way the sound of every other synth does; no wires are needed."}, {{"freq", "the frequency in Hz of the note (and of the last note after it ends). 440 before any note."}, {"note", "the note number: 60 is middle C, 69 is A 440, 0 to 127. mtof(note) is freq."}, {"gate", "1 while a key is held, 0 when it is let go. Several keys down: 1 until the last is let go."}, {"vel", "the velocity of the latest note, 0 to 1. It stays after the note ends."}, {"trig", "1 for the first sample of the block in which a note starts, else 0. A note played again while the key is held triggers again."}}},
     {"One copy for each voice, or one for the instrument", {"In front of a Voices node, each voice runs its own copy of the code, so each key has its own note, gate, History values and delay lines. This is how to make a polyphonic synth, a drum, or any sound a key starts and that ends by itself. How many copies there are is the instrument's Voices setting.", "After a Voices node, or in an instrument without one, there is a single copy that hears every note: the latest key down is the note, and gate is 1 while any key is held (a mono, last-note-priority synth). That is the right place for an effect that follows the played pitch, such as a pitch-following filter or a sample-and-hold.", "With no notes played (an effect on audio), freq is 440, gate 0, vel 1, note 69 and trig 0."}, {}},
     {"The language in a page", {"A program is a list of statements, one after another, run once for every sample. A statement ends with a semicolon. Comments are // to the end of a line and /* ... */."}, {{"x = a + b;", "sets x. A name you set is a variable for this sample only: it is not remembered for the next. Also += -= *= /=."}, {"a, b, c = x, y, z;", "sets several at once. All the values on the right are worked out first, then the names are set, so a, b = b, a; swaps them. One value for each name."}, {"History h(0);", "a value that is kept from one sample to the next, starting at the number given. h = h + 1; counts. Put them at the top, one to a line."}, {"Param name(def, min=0, max=1);", "a knob, p1 to p8 in the order declared. Read it by its name, never set it. Without min and max they are 0 and 1 (or the default if it is outside)."}, {"Delay d(size);", "a delay line of size samples (size can be samplerate or arithmetic on it). d.write(x); puts a value in, d.read(t) reads what was written t samples ago; t can be fractional."}, {"Data t(size);", "a buffer to read and write by index: peek(t, i) or t.read(i) reads, poke(t, v, i) or t.write(v, i) writes. The index wraps round. dim(t) is the size."}, {"if (a > b) { ... } else { ... }", "runs the first block while the condition is not 0, else the second. Declarations (History, Param, Delay, Data) go at the top, outside any if."}, {"c ? a : b", "a if c is not 0, else b."}, {"+ - * / % **", "the usual, also unary - and !. A division by 0 is 0; ** is a power; % is a remainder."}, {"== != < > <= >=  && ||", "comparisons give 1 or 0; && and || treat any number except 0 as true."}, {"in1  in2", "the audio coming in, left and right of what is wired to the node. Read only."}, {"out1  out2", "set them once a sample: the sound that goes out. If you do not set one it is silent."}, {"samplerate", "the engine's sample rate, a constant. The others are pi, twopi, halfpi, invpi, e, ln2, ln10, sqrt2 and sqrt1_2."}}},
     {"Operators", {"Hover a name in the code box for its help. Every one of these never makes a NaN or an infinity: where a calculation has no answer (a square root of a negative number, a log of 0) it gives 0."}, {{"trigonometry", "sin cos tan asin acos atan atan2 sinh cosh tanh. tanh is the soft clipper."}, {"rounding", "abs sign floor ceil round trunc fract"}, {"powers", "sqrt exp log log2 log10 pow"}, {"comparing and limiting", "min max clamp (clip) mix scale wrap fold mod"}, {"music", "mtof ftom (note number and Hz) dbtoa atodb (decibels and gain) mstosamps sampstoms (ms and samples)"}, {"generators", "phasor(hz) a ramp from 0 to 1; cycle(hz) a sine; noise() white noise between -1 and 1"}, {"followers", "slide(x, up, down) smooths x (up and down in samples); delta(x) the change since the last sample; change(x) its sign; dcblock(x) takes the DC offset out"}, {"safety", "fixdenorm(x) flushes tiny numbers to 0, which keeps feedback from slowing the CPU"}}},
     {"Recipes", {"An envelope for a key: env = slide(gate, mstosamps(attack), mstosamps(release)); and multiply it into the sound. Gate goes to 1 when the key is down: the envelope rises in attack ms; it falls in release ms when the key is let go.", "A drum that plays for the same time whatever the key does: amp = trig ? 1 : amp * 0.9995; (the envelope restarts at every note and falls by itself). Do not use gate.", "A velocity-sensitive tone: cutoff = 0.1 + 0.8 * vel; in a one-pole lowpass: lp = lp + (x - lp) * cutoff;", "Key tracking: scale the cutoff or an FM index with note, for example 0.2 + note / 127.", "A unison of oscillators: three phasor() calls with freq * (1 - d), freq and freq * (1 + d), where d is a few thousandths. Each call has its own phase.", "Add up the harmonics yourself for a saw that does not alias: a sum of sin(k * phase) / k for k up to about (samplerate / 2) / freq, with a fixed number of terms and a high note limit. There are no loops, so write the terms out or use a few.", "A sample-and-hold or a bit reducer: History held(0); History count(0); count = count + 1; if (count >= hold) { count = 0; held = in1; } (the Bitcrusher template).", "A tracker-style arpeggio: use the Arp node in front of the instrument (it works with this node like any other synth) instead of writing one in code."}, {}},
     {"The code box", {"Templates... at the top loads a working program, replacing the code in the box. Choose the last item, the code you had before, to go back one step (it appears after the first template is loaded).", "Compiling follows a moment after you stop typing. A successful edit starts the program again with all its History and Delay values at 0, so a sound that is playing restarts; a Param that is new, or whose default or range changed, moves its knob to its default. If there is a mistake, the code that was working keeps playing.", "Mistakes and warnings are listed under the box; click one to go to it. A warning does not stop anything: it says a Param is never used, or that nothing sets out1 or out2."}, {}}}},
  {"arp", "An arpeggiator. Put it in an instrument's graph (it has no wires) and the instrument no longer sounds held notes together: they become a pool, and the arp plays them one at a time, one step at a time, in the order the mode gives, locked to the song's tempo. It takes notes from patterns, the keyboard and MIDI alike.",
   {"Write a chord into one note column (or play one) and the arp does the rest. A single note repeats at the rate; two or three notes make the classic trance or acid pattern.", "Draw the step mask. The bars set each step's velocity and an empty bar is a rest, so a plain up arp becomes a gated, accented, offbeat line. Steps below 16 make the mask a different length from the bar, which gives rolling polyrhythms.", "Retrigger every new key for played parts. Retrigger every 16 steps keeps a long random or up/down pattern in step with the bar, whatever you play.", "Random modes follow the seed: change seed for a new line, and the same seed gives the same line every time. Random once with retrigger every 16 steps gives a different but repeating phrase per seed.", "interval cycle with one held note is the tracker 0xy arpeggio: note, +cycle1, +cycle2, on a fast rate with a short gate for a chiptune shimmer.", "Short gate with a plucky instrument is staccato; a gate above 100% with a slow envelope gives overlapping, legato notes. Octaves 2 or 3 with up/down+ is the big sweeping trance arp.", "Every parameter can be written from a pattern column or driven by a wire, so mode, rate, octaves and the transpose can change during the song: drop the rate for a build, switch to chord for the drop.", "Hold keeps the arp playing after you let go, so you can play it live and use both hands elsewhere. Pressing keys after letting go starts a new chord; turn hold off to stop.", "thru lets the played key sound too. Played on a low note with a high chord held it makes a bass note under an arp, though the arp instrument plays both, so for a separate bass use another instrument."},
   {"It arpeggiates the notes of its own instrument. It cannot send notes to another instrument, and one arp only is used per instrument (the first).", "Events are made once per audio block (about 3 ms), so a step may sit a few ms off its ideal place. It never drifts, but it is not sample-accurate.", "With the song stopped, the arp runs on its own clock at the song's tempo, so it cannot be in step with a song that starts later; start the song and it snaps to the song's grid.", "The velocity of the played key is one value for the whole chord (the last key pressed), not one per note.", "A tempo or rate change makes the steps re-align to the grid at the next block; a pattern may skip or repeat a step there.", "The notes it makes are limited to the 0 to 127 range, so a high chord with 4 octaves stops at the top."},
   {},
   {{"Modes", {"The pool is the held notes, repeated over the octaves. A mode is the order the pool is played in; the list repeats, so a pattern is as long as the order says. With a mask shorter or longer than the pattern the two drift against each other, which is useful."}, {{"up, down", "Low to high, or high to low."}, {"up/down, down/up", "Up then back down. The top and bottom notes play once: C E G E, C E G E..."}, {"up/down+, down/up+", "The same, but the top and bottom notes play twice at each turn: C E G G E C."}, {"converge", "From the outside in: lowest, highest, next lowest, next highest, until it meets in the middle."}, {"diverge", "The reverse: from the middle outward."}, {"converge/diverge", "In, then back out, without playing the ends twice."}, {"as played", "The order the keys went down, not the pitch order."}, {"random", "A free pick every step. Notes may repeat."}, {"random other", "Every note once in a random order, then a new order. No repeats until all have played."}, {"random once", "One random order, kept until the held notes change or the pattern restarts, then repeated."}, {"chord", "All the notes at once on every step. The step mask and rate make a rhythmic chord stab."}, {"repeat x2, repeat x4", "Each note, ascending, twice or four times before the next."}, {"pinky up", "The highest note between every note of an ascending line: C G, E G, (C G E G...). Bright and bouncy."}, {"pinky up/down", "The same, ascending and then back down."}, {"thumb up", "The lowest note between every note of an ascending line, so a bass pulse runs under the melody."}, {"thumb up/down", "The same, ascending and then back down."}, {"interval cycle", "Each held note, then it plus cycle1 semitones, then it plus cycle2. With one held note it is the tracker 0xy arpeggio."}}},
     {"Time", {"A step is `rate` ticks long. With the usual 4 ticks to the beat, 1 tick is a sixteenth note, 2 ticks an eighth, 4 ticks a quarter, and 1/2 tick a thirty-second. 2/3 and 1/3 tick make triplet-like grids. The arp reads the song's tempo and ticks per beat every block, so a tempo change in the master lane changes the arp too.", "The arp restarts at the start of the pattern in the cases `retrigger` lists. A new chord begins on the next step, so a key pressed between steps waits for the step: this keeps the arp tight. Swing delays every second step like the song's own swing."}, {}},
     {"The step mask", {"Click and drag in the bars under the node. A full bar is a step at full velocity, a half bar is half as loud, no bar is a rest. The steps use the arp's step count, beginning at the start of the song, so a mask like offbeat (..x...x...x...x.) plays the arp on the off-beat sixteenths and nothing else. The presets under the bars set common masks; random draws one."}, {}},
     {"Recipes", {}, {{"Trance arp", "Mode up/down+, rate 1 tick, octaves 3, gate 70%, mask 16ths, retrigger every note. Hold three notes (a minor chord) and bring in a long reverb."}, {"Acid line", "One held note per bar, mode up with octaves 2, mask gallop, gate 40%, a squelch instrument, retrigger every 16 steps."}, {"Offbeat stab", "Mode chord, rate 2 ticks, mask offbeat, gate 30%."}, {"Chiptune shimmer", "Mode interval cycle with cycle1 3 and cycle2 7 (minor), rate 1/2 tick, gate 80%."}, {"Strum", "Mode up, repeats 1, rate 1/4 tick, gate 100%+. One pass at speed, then silence until the next chord."}, {"Slow pad ripple", "Mode random other, rate 4 ticks, gate 150%, octaves 2, humanize 0.3."}}}}},
};

