  {"groove", "pops", "Now and then a loud pop with a low thump in it. 0 skips it."},
  {"groove", "surface", "The steady hiss of the vinyl itself. 0 skips it."},
  {"groove", "rumble", "The very low noise of the motor and the floor. 0 skips it."},
  {"groove", "mix", "Blend with the clean sound (delayed to match)."},
  {"groove", "quality", "standard works at the normal sample rate. simulation works the stylus at four times it and makes the clicks band-limited: less aliasing with loud, bright sounds, and a delay of about 30 samples (the clean sound is delayed to match)."},
  {"pulsar", "formant", "How fast each pulsaret plays, in Hz: the colour of the sound, a resonant peak that can sit anywhere, independent of the note. Under the note's pitch the pulsaret is cut off by the next one."},
  {"pulsar", "track", "How much the formant follows the note. 1 keeps the timbre the same up the keyboard; 0 leaves the peak where it is."},
  {"pulsar", "wave", "The waveform inside each pulsaret: sine, half sine, triangle, saw, square or noise."},
  {"pulsar", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones."},
  {"pulsar", "sweep", "How many octaves the drawn formant sweep moves the formant, at its top, after each note starts."},
  {"pulsar", "sweepms", "How long the formant sweep drawing takes, in ms."},
  {"pulsar", "mask", "The share of pulsarets dropped at random. Higher is sparser and more broken up."},
  {"pulsar", "jitter", "Random shifts to the time of each pulsaret: a little roughens the tone, a lot scatters it."},
  {"pulsar", "scatter", "Throws each pulsaret to a random place in the stereo field. 0 is mono."},
  {"pulsar", "glide", "Smooths a change of note over this many ms."},
  {"pulsar", "atk", "Attack time of the volume, in ms."},
  {"pulsar", "dec", "Decay time, in ms."},
  {"pulsar", "sus", "Sustain level."},
  {"pulsar", "rel", "Release time, in ms."},
  {"pulsar", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound."},
  {"pulsar", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"pulsetrain", "freq", "Pitch in Hz when track is off."},
  {"pulsetrain", "track", "On: the pitch follows the note. Off: it plays freq."},
  {"pulsetrain", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones."},
  {"pulsetrain", "formant", "How fast each pulsaret plays, in Hz: the colour of the sound."},
  {"pulsetrain", "wave", "The waveform inside each pulsaret: sine, half sine, triangle, saw, square or noise."},
  {"pulsetrain", "mask", "The share of pulsarets dropped at random."},
  {"pulsetrain", "jitter", "Random shifts to the time of each pulsaret."},
  {"pulsetrain", "scatter", "Throws each pulsaret to a random place in the stereo field. 0 is mono."},
  {"pulsetrain", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"clang", "shape", "From a thin round plate (0: cymbal, ride, bright and long) to a large hollow barrel (1: low, dull and boomy). It changes which resonances the body has and how they fade."},
  {"clang", "damping", "How fast the ringing dies (the damping ratio of the body). 0 rings for seconds, 1 is a dead clack."},
  {"clang", "highdamp", "How much faster the high resonances die than the low ones. Low keeps a long shimmer, high leaves only the dull low ring."},
  {"clang", "thump", "The weight of the hit: a low push with a falling pitch under the ring, and a little saturation to make it punch."},
  {"clang", "strike", "The beater. 0 is a soft mallet that only shakes the low resonances, 1 is a hard stick with a click that excites them all."},
  {"clang", "position", "Where the body is hit, centre to rim. It changes how loud each resonance is."},
  {"clang", "stretch", "Pushes the resonances further apart (above 1, bell-like) or closer together (below 1, more pipe-like)."},
  {"clang", "wash", "The sizzle of a cymbal: noise keeps feeding the high resonances after the hit."},
  {"clang", "width", "How far the resonances are spread over the stereo field."},
  {"clang", "tune", "Tuning in semitones."},
  {"clang", "vel", "How much the note velocity changes the level."},
  {"clang", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"clang", "release", "How fast the ringing is damped, in ms, after the note ends (a hand on the cymbal)."},
  {"membrane", "position", "Where the head is hit, from the middle (0) to the rim (1). The middle only shakes the round patterns (a deep boom); near the rim every pattern moves (a bright, ringing tone)."},
  {"membrane", "strike", "The beater. 0 is a soft mallet or a hand that only shakes the low modes, 1 is a hard stick with a click that shakes them all."},
  {"membrane", "damping", "How fast the ringing dies. 0 rings for seconds, 1 is a dead thud."},
  {"membrane", "highdamp", "How much faster the high modes die than the low ones. Low keeps a long shimmer, high leaves a dull fundamental."},
  {"membrane", "stiffness", "How far a stiff head pushes the high modes sharp, away from the pure drum ratios (1, 1.59, 2.14, 2.30, 2.65...). 0 is an ideal skin, high is a thick head or a plate."},
  {"membrane", "glide", "The pitch jump of a hit: the head tightens when it is struck and the pitch falls back in about 45 ms. The zap of a tom."},
  {"membrane", "shell", "The drum under the head: a deep, short note an octave below the lowest mode, with a small pitch drop (the boom of a kick)."},
  {"membrane", "width", "The two pick-ups listen at different angles round the head, so the modes with nodal lines sound different left and right."},
  {"membrane", "tune", "Tuning in semitones. The note is the pitch of the main tone (the second mode), which is what the ear takes as the pitch of a tom."},
  {"membrane", "vel", "How much the note velocity changes the level."},
  {"membrane", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"membrane", "release", "How fast the ringing is damped, in ms, after the note ends (a hand on the head)."},
  {"membrane", "quality", "Standard uses 40 modes. Eco uses the lowest 16: about half the CPU, and the missing high modes are quiet and short."},
  {"amp", "gain", "The preamp gain, 0 to 10. Low is clean, the middle is crunch, high is a saturated lead. The gain is spread over the stages, so it is driven harder in the later ones."},
  {"amp", "stages", "How many valve stages the preamp has, 1 to 4. One is open and clean; three or four are tight, compressed and sustaining at the same gain knob."},
  {"amp", "bright", "A lift in the treble in front of the gain, like a bright switch. It does most at low gain and fades as the gain goes up."},
  {"amp", "bias", "How lopsided the valve is. 0 is symmetrical (odd harmonics only, hard and fuzzy); more adds the even harmonics that make it warmer and fuller."},
  {"amp", "bass", "The tone stack, low end: 5 is flat, 0 and 10 take about 14 dB off or add it."},
  {"amp", "mid", "The tone stack, mids: 5 is flat; turn it down for a scooped sound, up for a cutting one."},
  {"amp", "treble", "The tone stack, treble: 5 is flat, 0 and 10 take about 14 dB off or add it."},
  {"amp", "voicing", "Where the three tone controls work: 0 scooped (clean, deep mids), 1 cutting (crunch, the mids forward), 2 chiming (bright, open)."},
  {"amp", "master", "How hard the power stage is driven, 0 to 10. Low is clean and loud; high adds a second layer of compression and distortion from the output stage."},
  {"amp", "sag", "How much the power stage gives when the signal is loud: the supply droops and the gain falls, so chords bloom and notes squash and recover. 0 is stiff."},
  {"amp", "presence", "The top end of the output stage, 0 to about +9 dB around 4.5 kHz. Adds air and bite after the distortion."},
  {"amp", "depth", "The low end of the output stage, 0 to about +8 dB around 95 Hz. Adds thump after the distortion."},
  {"amp", "cab", "The speaker cabinet: none, open 1x12 (airy and loose), closed 2x12 (tight and punchy) or stack 4x12 (big, dark and mid-focused). With none the sound is the raw amp, which is harsh: use it only when another node does the cabinet (a Convolver with an impulse response)."},
  {"amp", "cabtone", "Darker to brighter speaker: moves the top rolloff and the presence peak of the cabinet."},
  {"amp", "level", "Output level after everything. The amp changes loudness a lot with its settings, so set this last."},
  {"amp", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. 1 is all amp; a little dry sound keeps the attack of a pick through heavy distortion."},
  {"amp", "oversample", "Runs the two distortion stages at 1x, 2x or 4x to reduce aliasing (the rough, inharmonic fizz). Higher is cleaner and costs more CPU."},
  {"cardinal", "o1wave", "Wave of oscillator 1: triangle, saw, pulse, sine or noise."},
  {"cardinal", "o1pw", "Pulse width of oscillator 1 (only the pulse wave). 50% is a square."},
  {"cardinal", "o2wave", "Wave of oscillator 2."},
  {"cardinal", "o2pw", "Pulse width of oscillator 2."},
  {"cardinal", "o2semi", "Pitch of oscillator 2 against the note, in semitones. 7 makes a fifth, 12 an octave."},
  {"cardinal", "o2fine", "Fine tune of oscillator 2 in cents. A few cents against oscillator 1 thickens the sound."},
  {"cardinal", "o2track", "1: oscillator 2 follows the note. 0: it always plays the same pitch (use it with sync for a fixed formant)."},
  {"cardinal", "o2sync", "1: every cycle of oscillator 1 restarts oscillator 2. Sweep the pitch of oscillator 2 (with the mod envelope) for the classic sync lead."},
  {"cardinal", "fm", "How much oscillator 1 frequency-modulates oscillator 2: from a little brightness to metallic clang."},
  {"cardinal", "ring", "Ring modulation of the two oscillators, blended in. Gives bell-like and inharmonic sounds."},
  {"cardinal", "mix", "Balance of the oscillators: 0 is oscillator 1 alone, 1 is oscillator 2 alone."},
  {"cardinal", "noise", "White noise added to the oscillators."},
  {"cardinal", "unison", "How many detuned copies of the voice play together (1 to 5)."},
  {"cardinal", "detune", "How far apart the outermost unison copies are tuned, in cents."},
  {"cardinal", "width", "How wide the unison copies spread across the stereo field."},
  {"cardinal", "ftype", "Filter: lowpass 24 dB (with saturation), lowpass 12 dB, bandpass, highpass or notch."},
  {"cardinal", "cutoff", "Filter cutoff in Hz."},
  {"cardinal", "res", "Resonance: a peak at the cutoff. The 24 dB lowpass sings by itself at the top."},
  {"cardinal", "dist", "Distortion in front of the filter."},
  {"cardinal", "fkey", "How much the note moves the cutoff: 1 keeps the brightness the same on every note."},
  {"cardinal", "matk", "Mod envelope attack in ms."},
  {"cardinal", "mdec", "Mod envelope decay in ms."},
  {"cardinal", "msus", "Mod envelope sustain level."},
  {"cardinal", "mrel", "Mod envelope release in ms."},
  {"cardinal", "e2cut", "How far the mod envelope sweeps the cutoff, in octaves (negative sweeps down)."},
  {"cardinal", "e2pitch", "How far the mod envelope bends the pitch of oscillator 2, in semitones."},
  {"cardinal", "e2fm", "How much the mod envelope adds to the fm amount."},
  {"cardinal", "e2pw", "How much the mod envelope moves the pulse width."},
  {"cardinal", "atk", "Amplitude envelope attack in ms."},
  {"cardinal", "dec", "Amplitude envelope decay in ms."},
  {"cardinal", "sus", "Amplitude envelope sustain level."},
  {"cardinal", "rel", "Amplitude envelope release in ms."},
  {"cardinal", "vel", "How much note velocity changes the level."},
  {"cardinal", "l1wave", "LFO 1 wave: sine, triangle, saw, square or random (a new value each cycle)."},
  {"cardinal", "l1rate", "LFO 1 rate in Hz."},
  {"cardinal", "l1amt", "LFO 1 amount."},
  {"cardinal", "l1dest", "What LFO 1 moves: both pitches (vibrato), pitch 2, fm, pulse width, filter or level."},
  {"cardinal", "l2wave", "LFO 2 wave."},
  {"cardinal", "l2rate", "LFO 2 rate in Hz."},
  {"cardinal", "l2amt", "LFO 2 amount."},
  {"cardinal", "l2dest", "What LFO 2 moves."},
  {"cardinal", "glide", "Smooths a note into the next, in ms."},
  {"cardinal", "slide", "A straight-line glide from the previous note of the same column, in ticks."},
  {"cardinal", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"cardinal", "quality", "standard is the full sound. eco works out the slow pitch, fm and pulse-width moves every 8 samples instead of every sample: roughly a fifth less CPU for a busy lead, and the difference is very hard to hear. Use it while writing, or for a song that is close to the limit."},
  {"range", "lo", "The real value the port gets when the input is at 0 (Hz, ms, dB ... as the port it feeds uses them)."},
  {"range", "hi", "The real value the port gets when the input is at 1. On a log port such as a frequency the input sweeps evenly across octaves between lo and hi."},
  {"range", "at", "Where between lo and hi the output sits while nothing is wired into the input: a knob in real units."},
  {"add", "value", "Added to the signal at control rate. Negative values lower it."},
  {"times", "by", "The signal is multiplied by this (and by the mod input, if wired). Negative flips it."},
  {"int", "value", "A whole number as a control signal. Type it, drag it, or use the + and - buttons."},
  {"float", "value", "A number as a control signal. Type it or drag it."},
  {"drumsynth", "startPitch", "Pitch in Hz at the top of the pitch envelope, at the root note: where the hit begins."},
  {"drumsynth", "endPitch", "Pitch in Hz the body falls to as the pitch envelope dies away: the note you hear in the tail."},
  {"drumsynth", "pitchMul", "Multiplies both pitches, to tune the whole hit up or down without changing its sweep. 2 is an octave up."},
  {"drumsynth", "keytrack", "1: the note played moves the pitches. 0: they stay as set."},
  {"drumsynth", "root", "The note at which the pitches play as written."},
  {"drumsynth", "patk", "Pitch envelope attack in ms. Keep it tiny for a drop from the top."},
  {"drumsynth", "pdec", "Pitch envelope decay in ms: how fast the pitch falls from the start pitch to the end pitch."},
  {"drumsynth", "psus", "Pitch envelope sustain while the note is held: how far up the pitch stays. 0 for a plain drop."},
  {"drumsynth", "prel", "Pitch envelope release in ms."},
  {"drumsynth", "body", "Level of the pitched part of the hit."},
  {"drumsynth", "attack", "Volume envelope attack in ms."},
  {"drumsynth", "decay", "Volume envelope decay in ms: how long the body rings."},
  {"drumsynth", "sustain", "Volume envelope sustain while the note is held. 0 lets a hit end by itself."},
  {"drumsynth", "release", "Volume envelope release in ms."},
  {"drumsynth", "drive", "Rounds the peaks of the whole hit for a harder, denser sound."},
  {"drumsynth", "noise", "How much noise is added to the hit."},
  {"drumsynth", "noiseType", "The colour of the noise: white (bright, even), pink (softer), brown (deep rumble), blue (thin, hissy), violet (thinner still)."},
  {"drumsynth", "noiseTone", "Low-pass on the noise in Hz: lower takes the sizzle off."},
  {"drumsynth", "natk", "Noise envelope attack in ms."},
  {"drumsynth", "ndec", "Noise envelope decay in ms: how long the noise lasts."},
  {"drumsynth", "nsus", "Noise envelope sustain while the note is held."},
  {"drumsynth", "nrel", "Noise envelope release in ms."},
  {"drumsynth", "click", "How much of the click, the short burst that starts the hit, is added."},
  {"drumsynth", "clickMode", "synthesized: the click is made by the node. sample: it is the start of a sample from this node's list."},
  {"drumsynth", "clickLen", "Length of the click in ms (for a sample, how much of its start is played)."},
  {"drumsynth", "clickTone", "Pitch of the synthesized click in Hz. Not used by a sample click."},
  {"drumsynth", "clickSample", "Which sample of the node's list is the click (00 is the first). Only used when the click is a sample."},
  {"drumsynth", "clickTail", "Smooths the end of a sample click: it fades out over this many ms so a cut-off sample does not add a tick of its own. Only used when the click is a sample."},
  {"drumsynth", "vel", "How much the note velocity changes the level."},
  {"drumsynth", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"oscout", "mode", "0: Renoise's own OSC addresses (/renoise/trigger/note_on and note_off, so Wax can play a Renoise song's instruments). 1: Wax's addresses, /wax/NAME/note_on and note_off with the MIDI note and velocity."},
  {"oscout", "instrument", "Renoise: the instrument number to play, counted from 0 as its OSC server counts."},
  {"oscout", "track", "Renoise: the track number (from 0), or -1 for the current track."},
  {"oscout", "transpose", "Semitones added to every note sent."},
  {"comb", "freq", "The first peak of the comb, in Hz; the others sit at its multiples. Feed it noise or clicks and it plays this pitch."},
  {"comb", "feedback", "Sends the output back through the delay: higher rings longer (a tuned resonator). Negative puts the peaks between the harmonics and sounds an octave lower."},
  {"comb", "ff", "Mixes in the delayed input once, with no ringing: notches at the odd multiples (positive) or at the multiples (negative)."},
  {"comb", "damp", "Darkens the feedback path, so the high peaks die first, like a string."},
  {"comb", "spread", "Detunes the two channels in opposite directions, in cents, for width."},
  {"comb", "mix", "Dry to comb. 1 is all comb."},
  {"clap", "tone", "Centre of the band-pass the noise goes through, in Hz. Higher is thinner and snappier."},
  {"clap", "q", "Narrowness of the band-pass. Higher is more pitched and ringy; low is breathy."},
  {"clap", "bursts", "How many quick hits come before the tail: the individual hands."},
  {"clap", "spacing", "Milliseconds between the bursts."},
  {"clap", "burstDecay", "How fast each burst dies, in ms to fall by 40 dB. Short is tight, longer smears them together."},
  {"clap", "tailDecay", "Length of the tail, in ms to fall by 40 dB."},
  {"clap", "tailLevel", "How loud the tail is against the bursts."},
  {"clap", "spread", "How different the left and right noise are. 0 is mono; higher is wider."},
  {"clap", "drive", "Rounds the peaks for a denser, harder clap."},
  {"clap", "vel", "How much the digit of a hit changes its level."},
  {"clap", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"urugraVerb", "predelay", "Milliseconds before the reverb starts. It sits inside the comb loops, so it also stretches each echo of the tail."},
  {"urugraVerb", "size", "Room size: 0 is small and tight, 1 is large."},
  {"urugraVerb", "decay", "How long the tail rings. Used from 0.1 to 0.93."},
  {"urugraVerb", "damp", "Darkens the tail: higher is duller and the highs die sooner."},
  {"urugraVerb", "mod", "Depth of a very slow (0.11 Hz) wobble in the delay times, which keeps the tail from ringing."},
  {"urugraVerb", "dry", "Level of the unprocessed sound."},
  {"urugraVerb", "wet", "Level of the reverb."},
  {"urugraVerb", "width", "Stereo width of the reverb: 0 is mono, 1 as it is, 2 wide."},
  {"convolver", "sample", "Which of the node's samples is the impulse response. Add one under the graph (the Add sample button); a pattern's Smp column can switch between several."},
  {"convolver", "size", "Stretches the response: below 1 is a smaller, higher-pitched room, above 1 a bigger, lower one."},
  {"convolver", "decay", "Fades the response out over this many seconds. 10 leaves it as recorded."},
  {"convolver", "predelay", "Milliseconds before the reverb starts, on top of the 3 ms the node always takes."},
  {"convolver", "lowcut", "Removes the lows from the reverb (it keeps the dry sound whole)."},
  {"convolver", "highcut", "Removes the highs from the reverb, for a darker room."},
  {"convolver", "width", "Stereo width of the reverb: 0 is mono, 1 as recorded, above 1 wider."},
  {"convolver", "level", "Level of the reverb in dB. The response is evened out, so different ones come in at about the same level."},
  {"convolver", "mix", "Dry to reverb. 0 is dry only."},
  {"sampler", "level", "Output level of the sampler."},
  {"sampler", "root", "The note (0 to 127, 60 is middle C) at which the sample plays at its own pitch. Notes above or below speed it up or slow it down from here."},
  {"sampler", "release", "Fade-out in ms when a note ends, for looping samples (one-shots play to their end)."},
  {"sampler", "nnaFade", "New-note action: when a new note starts in a column, the note that column was playing fades out over this many ms (0 cuts it at once). Looping samples always fade; one-shots too unless nnaAll is 0."},
  {"sampler", "offset", "Where the note starts in the sample, as a fraction of its length (0 start, 1 end). With reverse on it is measured from the end."},
  {"sampler", "reverse", "1 plays the sample backwards."},
  {"sampler", "retrig", "Repeats the note every this many ticks (0 is no repeats). In a pattern, hex 1000 is one tick."},
  {"sampler", "repeats", "How many repeats `retrig` makes (0 means until the note ends)."},
  {"sampler", "shift", "How far each repeat starts further along the sample, as a fraction of it (negative goes back). Repeats stop when they run off the sample. In a pattern, hex 8000 is zero."},
  {"sampler", "ramp", "What each repeat's volume is multiplied by. Below 1 the repeats fade out; above 1 they swell. In a pattern, hex C000 is 1."},
  {"sampler", "sample", "Which sample of the instrument's list plays (0 is the first)."},
  {"sampler", "slice", "Start the note at this slice marker of the sample. A number past the last marker turns slicing off and uses `offset` instead."},
  {"sampler", "grain", "How long each sound lasts, in ticks (0 plays the sample to its end). With a grain length the repeats overlap instead of cutting each other, so a short interval and a long grain make a cloud. In a pattern, hex 1000 is one tick."},
  {"sampler", "edge", "How much of a grain is fade in and fade out: 0 is hard edges (2 ms, to stop clicks), 1 is a triangle. Only with a grain length."},
  {"sampler", "accel", "Each repeat's interval is multiplied by this: below 1 the repeats speed up, above 1 they slow down. In a pattern, hex 8000 is 1."},
  {"sampler", "step", "How many semitones each repeat is higher than the one before (negative is lower): arpeggios and glides from one note. In a pattern, hex 8000 is 0 and 0800 is one semitone."},
  {"sampler", "slide", "Bends the pitch while a sound plays, in semitones per tick (negative falls). In a pattern, hex 8000 is 0."},
  {"sampler", "flip", "1 makes every repeat play the other way round from the one before."},
  {"sampler", "prob", "The chance that a repeat sounds, 0 to 1. The timing carries on, so a missed repeat is a gap. In a pattern, hex FFFF is always."},
  {"sampler", "jOffset", "Random start position: each sound starts up to this fraction of the sample before or after where it should."},
  {"sampler", "jPitch", "Random pitch: each sound is up to this many semitones higher or lower."},
  {"sampler", "jPan", "Random pan: each sound is placed up to this far to the left or right of the sample's own pan."},
  {"sampler", "jTime", "Each repeat comes late by a random part of the interval, up to this fraction. It only delays, so the average rate slows a little."},
  {"sampler", "jLevel", "Random volume: each sound is quieter by a random amount, up to this fraction."},
  {"sampler", "volLin", "Volume slide: XXYY, a linear slide of the sound to the level YY/40 (hex; 40 is the sound's own level, 20 half, 80 double) in XX subticks (six to a tick; 00 is at once). Written while a sound plays it slides that sound; before a note, every sound of the note. 0000 is off."},
  {"sampler", "volLog", "Volume slide: like volLin, but the slide is even in dB, so it sounds like a steady fade. A target of 00 slides to about -80 dB."},
  {"sampler", "volDown", "Volume slide down: XXYY, a logarithmic slide down by YY dB in XX subticks."},
  {"sampler", "volUp", "Volume slide up: XXYY, a logarithmic slide up by YY dB in XX subticks."},
  {"sampler", "volFineDown", "Fine volume slide down: like volDown, but YY is in 256ths of a dB."},
  {"sampler", "volFineUp", "Fine volume slide up: like volUp, but YY is in 256ths of a dB."},
  {"sampler", "volDownMs", "Like volDown, but the time is 10 * XX milliseconds instead of subticks."},
  {"sampler", "volUpMs", "Like volUp, but the time is 10 * XX milliseconds."},
  {"sampler", "volFineDownMs", "Like volFineDown, but the time is 10 * XX milliseconds."},
  {"sampler", "volFineUpMs", "Like volFineUp, but the time is 10 * XX milliseconds."},
  {"sampler", "subdivide", "How many subticks make a tick (default 6). The time of the volume slides is counted in subticks, so a bigger number makes the slides shorter and finer."},
  {"sampler", "blockCount", "Absolute start: below 65535 the Off column is the low four hex digits of a frame number and this is how many full blocks of 65536 frames come before it (command FF); 65535 is the usual fraction of the sample (command F0)."},
  {"sampler", "portDown", "Slides the pitch down: XXYY is XX semitones over YY subticks. Written while a sound plays it slides that sound; before a note, every sound of the note."},
  {"sampler", "portUp", "Slides the pitch up: XXYY is XX semitones over YY subticks."},
  {"sampler", "portFineDown", "Like the pitch slide down, in 256ths of a semitone: 0680 is half a semitone over eight subticks."},
  {"sampler", "portFineUp", "Like the pitch slide up, in 256ths of a semitone."},
  {"sampler", "portTo", "Glide: a note that follows one still sounding in its column bends to the new pitch over XXY.Y subticks instead of restarting (0000: restart). Needs the notes in the same note column."},
  {"sampler", "retrigMin", "The shortest time between repeats, XX.YY subticks (0000: no limit)."},
  {"sampler", "retrigMax", "The longest time between repeats, XXYY subticks (0000: no limit)."},
  {"sampler", "retrigPow", "Each interval is also multiplied by n to this power, n counting the repeats: 8000 is none, C000 makes them lengthen like n, 4000 shorten like 1/n."},
  {"sampler", "cutAfter", "Every sound is cut after this many ticks: XXX.Y in hex, 0010 is one tick, 0018 one and a half. 0000: never."},
  {"sampler", "releaseAfter", "Every sound is released after this many ticks (as for Cut after) and fades over the Release time."},
  {"sampler", "cutScale", "Each repeat multiplies the cut and release times by this: 8000 is x1, 4000 halves them, C000 makes them 1.5 times longer."},
  {"sampler", "stretchSub", "Stretches the whole sample over xxyy-8000 subticks (8000: off): each repeat starts as far along as the time since the last one, so the sound plays at its own pitch but over that length. Negative goes backwards (set Off to FFFF to start at the end). Needs a repeat time (Rtg)."},
  {"sampler", "stretchTicks", "Like the subticks stretch, with the length in ticks (xxyy-8000)."},
  {"sampler", "stretchKeep", "Like the ticks stretch, and the length holds when the repeat time changes (accel, limits, curve), so the sample is always spread over the same time."},
  {"sampler", "jumpAhead", "Jumps XXYY subticks ahead in the wave of every sound that is playing, whatever its playing speed."},
  {"sampler", "jumpBack", "Jumps XXYY subticks back in the wave, whatever the playing speed."},
  {"sampler", "jumpAheadRel", "Jumps XXYY subticks ahead, counted in the sound's playing speed (a sound an octave up jumps twice as far)."},
  {"sampler", "jumpBackRel", "Jumps XXYY subticks back, by the playing speed."},
  {"sampler", "jumpTicksAhead", "Like the subtick jump ahead, in ticks."},
  {"sampler", "jumpTicksBack", "Like the subtick jump back, in ticks."},
  {"sampler", "jumpTicksAheadRel", "Like the jump ahead by speed, in ticks."},
  {"sampler", "jumpTicksBackRel", "Like the jump back by speed, in ticks."},
  {"sampler", "filterType", "A filter on each sound: 0 off, 1 low pass, 2 high pass, 3 band pass (constant skirt gain), 4 band pass (constant peak gain), 5 notch."},
  {"sampler", "filterCutoff", "The filter cutoff, 0000 (20 Hz) to FFFF (20 kHz), evenly in pitch."},
  {"sampler", "filterRes", "The filter resonance: 0000 flat, FFFF very resonant."},
  {"sampler", "cutDown", "Cutoff slide down: XXYY, XX semitones over YY subticks. Written while a sound plays it slides that sound."},
  {"sampler", "cutUp", "Cutoff slide up: XXYY, XX semitones over YY subticks."},
  {"sampler", "cutStep", "Semitones each repeat moves the filter cutoff: 8000 none, 0800 one semitone up."},
  {"sampler", "jCutoff", "Humanize: each sound gets a random cutoff, up to XX.YY semitones either way."},
  {"sampler", "jTimeSub", "Humanize: each repeat comes late by a random time up to XX.YY subticks."},
  {"sampler", "vibSpeed", "Vibrato: the period in subticks, XXY.Y (0060 is six subticks, one tick). 0 turns it off."},
  {"sampler", "vibDepth", "Vibrato depth, XX.YY semitones (0100 is one)."},
  {"sampler", "vibWave", "Vibrato wave: 0 sine, 1 triangle, 2 square, 3 saw."},
  {"sampler", "lfoSpeed", "Cutoff LFO: the period in subticks, XXY.Y. 0 off. Needs the filter on."},
  {"sampler", "lfoDepth", "Cutoff LFO depth, XX.YY semitones of cutoff."},
  {"sampler", "lfoWave", "Cutoff LFO wave: 0 sine, 1 triangle, 2 square, 3 saw."},
  {"sampler", "vibPhase", "Every new note starts its vibrato at this phase, XXYY of a turn; FFFF lets it run free."},
  {"sampler", "lfoPhase", "Every new note starts the cutoff LFO at this phase; FFFF lets it run free."},
  {"sampler", "vibSync", "Sets the vibrato phase of every sound now, XXYY of a turn (4000 is a quarter)."},
  {"sampler", "lfoSync", "Sets the cutoff LFO phase of every sound now."},
  {"osc", "freq", "Pitch in Hz, used only when `track` is 0. With track on, the note sets the pitch."},
  {"osc", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones."},
  {"osc", "wave", "Waveform: 0 sine, 1 saw, 2 square, 3 triangle, 4 pulse (its width is `width`), 5 half sine, 6 rectified sine, 7 steps (a saw in eight steps), 8 drawn (the single cycle you draw in the panel). Everything but the sine and triangle has corners and is not band-limited, so it aliases at high pitch."},
  {"osc", "track", "1: the pitch follows the note played. 0: a fixed pitch from `freq`."},
  {"osc", "reset", "1: every note restarts the wave at `phase`, so each hit sounds the same (what a bass drum wants). 0: the oscillator free-runs."},
  {"osc", "phase", "Where a restart begins in the cycle, from 0 to 1."},
  {"osc", "level", "Output level."},
  {"osc", "width", "How much of the cycle the pulse wave (4) is high, from a thin 0.02 to a wide 0.98; 0.5 is a square. Move it for the hollow, nasal sound of pulse width modulation."},
  {"noise", "level", "Level of the noise. The colours are matched in loudness, so tilt changes the tone, not the level."},
  {"noise", "tilt", "The colour of the noise, as the slope of its spectrum in dB per octave: -6 brown (a deep rumble), -3 pink (even across the octaves, like rain or a waterfall), 0 white, +3 blue, +6 violet (a thin hiss). Values between blend neighbours."},
  {"noise", "seed", "Which noise it is. Another number is another texture, the same every hit."},
  {"noise", "restart", "1: every note starts the noise over from the seed, so every hit is identical. 0: the noise runs on and each hit differs."},
  {"noise", "free", "1: the noise runs by itself, as a bed under other sounds. 0: it is silent until a note."},
  {"env", "attack", "Attack in ms: how long the envelope takes to rise to its peak after a note. Every note played on this instrument starts it."},
  {"env", "decay", "Decay in ms: how long it takes to fall from the peak to the sustain level (to fall by 60 dB)."},
  {"env", "sustain", "The level held while the note is held, from 0 to 1 of the peak. At 0 the envelope dies away after the decay."},
  {"env", "release", "Release in ms: how long it takes to fall back to 0 after the note ends."},
  {"env", "amount", "The size of the swing: the output is the envelope times this. When the envelope drives a filter's `mod` or an oscillator's `fm`, it is the number of octaves."},
  {"env", "vel", "How much the note's velocity scales the envelope: 0 ignores velocity, 1 follows it fully."},
  {"lfo", "rate", "Speed in Hz."},
  {"lfo", "shape", "0 sine, 1 triangle, 2 saw, 3 square."},
  {"lfo", "amount", "Size of the output swing (the wave times this). On a filter's `mod` or an oscillator's `fm` it is in octaves."},
  {"lfo", "reset", "1: every note restarts the LFO from the beginning. 0: it runs freely."},
  {"lfo", "range", "0: the wave swings either side of zero by Amount. 1: the wave runs between Min and Max instead, so it can drive a parameter within the range you choose (Amount is ignored)."},
  {"lfo", "min", "The lowest value the LFO reaches, when Range is 1. Can be negative."},
  {"lfo", "max", "The highest value the LFO reaches, when Range is 1. Set it below Min to flip the wave upside down."},
  {"lfo", "steps", "How many times per tick the LFO is worked out; between updates it holds its value. 0 does it for every sample. Wired to a parameter, 8 to 24 sounds smooth and costs far less than 0."},
  {"gain", "gain", "Multiplies the level of the signal (1 leaves it unchanged)."},
  {"mix", "mix", "Blend of its two inputs: 0 is all of a, 1 is all of b."},
  {"pan", "pan", "Stereo position: -1 hard left, 0 centre, 1 hard right."},
  {"filter", "cutoff", "Cutoff frequency in Hz. Wire an envelope or LFO into `mod` to sweep it (the mod input is in octaves)."},
  {"filter", "resonance", "Emphasis at the cutoff. Low is smooth; high rings and whistles around the cutoff."},
  {"filter", "mode", "0 lowpass (keeps lows, cuts highs), 1 bandpass (keeps a band around the cutoff), 2 highpass (keeps highs, cuts lows)."},
  {"filterpro", "cutoff", "Cutoff in Hz. For the vowel mode it slides all formants (1000 Hz is neutral); for the comb modes it is the pitch the comb rings at. Wire an envelope or LFO into `mod` (octaves, sample by sample)."},
  {"filterpro", "resonance", "Emphasis at the cutoff. Near 1 the filter sings by itself. In the comb modes it is the feedback; in vowel it narrows the formants."},
  {"filterpro", "mode", "The voicing: lowpass, bandpass, highpass, notch, peak, allpass, morph, 24 dB lowpass, Moog-style ladder, vowel (a e i o u formants), comb + and comb - (a tuned ringing delay)."},
  {"filterpro", "morph", "Only for the morph mode: sweeps from lowpass (0) through notch (0.5) to highpass (1)."},
  {"filterpro", "vowel", "Only for the vowel mode: 0 a, 1 e, 2 i, 3 o, 4 u, and the values between blend neighbours."},
  {"filterpro", "damp", "Only for the comb modes: darkens the ringing so highs die faster."},
  {"filterpro", "drive", "Saturation before the filter. More bites and thickens; the level is evened out for you."},
  {"filterpro", "bias", "Pushes the signal off centre before the saturation for lopsided, even-harmonic grit (needs some drive to show)."},
  {"filterpro", "mix", "Dry/wet balance: 0 is the untouched input, 1 is all filtered."},
  {"filterpro", "level", "Output level."},
  {"twin", "drive", "Saturation in front of both filters. More bites and thickens; the level is evened out for you."},
  {"twin", "tilt", "Tilts the spectrum before the filters: positive brightens, negative darkens, around the tilt frequency."},
  {"twin", "tiltfreq", "The pivot of the tilt."},
  {"twin", "trigger", "The level (dB) at which the follower starts to open the filters."},
  {"twin", "cutoff1", "Cutoff of filter 1 in Hz. Wire an envelope or LFO into mod1 (octaves)."},
  {"twin", "res1", "Emphasis of filter 1. Near 1 it sings by itself; the band state is soft-clipped so it stays bounded."},
  {"twin", "low1", "Level of filter 1's low pass, from -1 to 1. Mix low, band and high (negative values too) for notches and peaks."},
  {"twin", "band1", "Level of filter 1's band pass, from -1 to 1."},
  {"twin", "high1", "Level of filter 1's high pass, from -1 to 1."},
  {"twin", "cutoff2", "Cutoff of filter 2 in Hz when the lock is free. Wire an envelope or LFO into mod2 (octaves)."},
  {"twin", "res2", "Emphasis of filter 2."},
  {"twin", "low2", "Level of filter 2's low pass, from -1 to 1."},
  {"twin", "band2", "Level of filter 2's band pass, from -1 to 1."},
  {"twin", "high2", "Level of filter 2's high pass, from -1 to 1."},
  {"twin", "blend", "Series to parallel: 0 sends the input through filter 1 and then filter 2; 1 runs them side by side and adds them."},
  {"twin", "lock", "Free: filter 2 has its own cutoff. Ratio: filter 2 sits at the ratio times filter 1's cutoff, so moving one moves both."},
  {"twin", "ratio", "The lock ratio, from a quarter to four times. 2 is an octave above, 1.5 a fifth, 1.25 a major third."},
  {"twin", "detune", "Nudges filter 2 away from the exact ratio, in cents."},
  {"twin", "track", "Follow: the cutoffs follow the pitch heard in the input (monophonic sounds work best). With the lock on, filter 1 sits at the pitch divided by the ratio."},
  {"twin", "transpose", "Shifts the followed pitch in semitones."},
  {"twin", "lforate", "Speed of the built-in LFO in Hz."},
  {"twin", "lfoshape", "LFO wave: sine, triangle, saw down or square."},
  {"twin", "lfo1", "How far the LFO swings filter 1's cutoff, up to 2 octaves either side."},
  {"twin", "lfo2", "How far the LFO swings filter 2's cutoff."},
  {"twin", "fattack", "How quickly the follower rises with the input level, in ms."},
  {"twin", "frelease", "How slowly the follower falls, in ms."},
  {"twin", "fsens", "How much input level it takes to move the follower."},
  {"twin", "fol1", "How far the follower opens filter 1 (up to 3 octaves; negative closes it)."},
  {"twin", "fol2", "How far the follower opens filter 2."},
  {"twin", "noise", "Adds noise to the input so the filters have something to sing with."},
  {"twin", "feedback", "Feeds the output back to the input, for ringing and self-oscillation."},
  {"twin", "fbsource", "Where the feedback is taken from: filter 1 alone, or the final output."},
  {"twin", "mix", "Dry/wet balance: 0 is the untouched input."},
  {"twin", "level", "Output level."},
  {"ladder", "cutoff", "Centre of the sweep in Hz. The filter is a 24 dB lowpass in the classic ladder style."},
  {"ladder", "resonance", "Emphasis at the cutoff. Near 1 the filter sings by itself."},
  {"ladder", "drive", "Saturation in front of the poles. More bites and thickens; the level is evened out for you."},
  {"ladder", "rate", "Speed of the built-in LFO in Hz, on a log scale so the slow end (a sweep every minute or so) has the most room."},
  {"ladder", "depth", "How far the LFO swings the cutoff, in octaves either side. 0 turns the LFO off."},
  {"ladder", "shape", "LFO wave: sine, triangle, saw down or square."},
  {"ladder", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. Turn it from 0 to 1 while playing to morph the filter in smoothly; the control is smoothed so it never clicks."},
  {"ladder", "level", "Output level."},
  {"ladder", "phase", "Where the LFO is in its cycle (a whole turn from 0 to 1). Shifts the sweep against the music, or against another filter's LFO."},
  {"sweep", "sweep", "One knob, filtering as if it were always part of the signal. 0 leaves the sound untouched; turning down closes a lowpass from the top, turning up opens a highpass from the bottom. The filter fades in over the first few percent, so there is no dry/wet seam. Wire an LFO or envelope into `mod` (a full swing is 1.0) for an auto-filter."},
  {"sweep", "resonance", "Emphasis at the cutoff. It grows in with the distance from the middle, so the centre stays clean."},
  {"sweep", "slope", "12 dB for a gentle slope, 24 dB for a steeper one."},
  {"genexpr", "p1", "Knob 1 of the code: the first Param declared in the code box. It runs 0 to 1 here and is stretched to that Param's own min and max. The code panel lists what each knob is."},
  {"genexpr", "p2", "Knob 2: the second Param declared in the code, 0 to 1, stretched to its min and max."},
  {"genexpr", "p3", "Knob 3: the third Param declared in the code, 0 to 1, stretched to its min and max."},
  {"genexpr", "p4", "Knob 4: the fourth Param declared in the code, 0 to 1, stretched to its min and max."},
  {"genexpr", "p5", "Knob 5: the fifth Param declared in the code, 0 to 1, stretched to its min and max."},
  {"genexpr", "p6", "Knob 6: the sixth Param declared in the code, 0 to 1, stretched to its min and max."},
  {"genexpr", "p7", "Knob 7: the seventh Param declared in the code, 0 to 1, stretched to its min and max."},
  {"genexpr", "p8", "Knob 8: the eighth Param declared in the code, 0 to 1, stretched to its min and max."},
  {"vocoder", "bands", "How many frequency bands the spectrum is split into. More bands make speech clearer, fewer sound more robotic."},
  {"vocoder", "low", "Lowest band centre in Hz."},
  {"vocoder", "high", "Highest band centre in Hz."},
  {"vocoder", "q", "Band sharpness. Higher separates the bands more (more articulate, thinner); lower blends them."},
  {"vocoder", "attack", "How fast a band opens when the voice gets louder, in ms."},
  {"vocoder", "release", "How fast a band closes when the voice gets quieter, in ms. Short is choppy and clear, long is smeared."},
  {"vocoder", "shift", "Moves the carrier bands up or down against the voice bands, in semitones: a formant shift, for chipmunk or giant voices."},
  {"vocoder", "sibilance", "Lets the highs of the voice (s, t, f) straight through so consonants stay understandable."},
  {"vocoder", "tune", "Pitch of the built-in buzz carrier in Hz. Only used when nothing is wired to `carrier`. Wire a synth or chord into `carrier` instead for a played vocoder."},
  {"vocoder", "noise", "Noise mixed into the built-in carrier, for whispery, breathy consonants."},
  {"vocoder", "level", "Output level."},
  {"tap", "trig", "Write 1 in a pattern to start a take on that row, 0 to end it early. Every write to the cell counts, so 1 on a later row retakes."},
  {"tap", "len", "Length of a take in ticks (pattern rows). 0 records until a 0 arrives in trig, or the song stops (at most two minutes)."},
  {"tap", "smp", "Which pool sample the take is written to: 1 to 16 is tap01 to tap16. 0 records nothing. A sampler plays it by picking the same tapNN as its sample."},
  {"tap", "ch", "1 records mono, 2 records stereo."},
  {"tap", "fade", "Fades the end of the take to silence over this many ms, so it never clicks when it loops or is played back."},
  {"tap", "fadein", "Fades the start of the take in over this many ms."},
  {"tap", "copy", "Also writes the take into this normal sample (its number in the sample list). 0 does not copy. A number past the end of the list adds a new sample."},
  {"delay", "time", "Delay time in ms (up to one second)."},
  {"delay", "feedback", "How much of the delayed sound is fed back, for repeats. Higher gives more echoes."},
  {"delay", "mix", "Dry/wet balance: 0 is no echo, 1 is all echo."},
  {"chorus", "rate", "Speed of the wobble in Hz."},
  {"chorus", "depth", "How far the delay swings, in ms. More is a stronger, seasick detune."},
  {"chorus", "delay", "Centre delay time in ms. Longer sounds more like a doubled voice, shorter more like a flanger."},
  {"chorus", "voices", "How many modulated copies are added (1 to 4). More is thicker."},
  {"chorus", "width", "Offsets the right channel's wobble from the left for stereo width. 0 is mono."},
  {"chorus", "mix", "Amount of the chorused sound added to the dry sound."},
  {"chorus", "phase", "Where in its cycle the sweep restarts, 0 to 1. Nothing happens until a pattern writes it (expose it as a column): then the sweep slides to this point and runs on from there, so it can be lined up with the beat."},
  {"chorus", "glide", "Milliseconds the sweep takes to slide to a phase written by a pattern. 0 jumps at once, which clicks unless the sound is silent (set it as a note starts)."},
  {"pogue", "pregain", "Level into the effect, in dB. The dry sound is boosted or cut too; more drive into saturation and degrade."},
  {"pogue", "voices", "How many delayed copies read the sound back (1 to 8). More is thicker."},
  {"pogue", "rate", "Speed of the fastest voice, in Hz. Each voice moves at its own speed, between a third of this and this."},
  {"pogue", "resolution", "How many times a second the voices are moved. Turn it down for a stepped, zippery wobble."},
  {"pogue", "change", "How random the movement is. 0 is smooth sines, 1 wanders between random delays."},
  {"pogue", "mindelay", "The shortest delay a voice reaches, in ms."},
  {"pogue", "maxdelay", "The longest delay a voice reaches, in ms. A wide range between the two is a deep, seasick chorus."},
  {"pogue", "stereo", "Mono: both sides move together. Wide: each side has its own voices. Spread: each voice is panned somewhere across the field."},
  {"pogue", "interp", "How the delay is read between samples. None is gritty and steppy, linear is smooth, cubic is cleanest."},
  {"pogue", "feedback", "Wet sound sent back into its own side. Negative inverts it, for a hollower sound."},
  {"pogue", "xfeedback", "Wet sound sent back into the other side, so the two sides bleed into each other."},
  {"pogue", "saturation", "Soft clipping on the wet sound. With feedback it grows and grinds."},
  {"pogue", "degrade", "Lowers the bit depth of the wet sound, from clean (0) to 2 bits (1)."},
  {"pogue", "dither", "Feeds the rounding error of degrade back into the next sample: the grit moves up into a hiss."},
  {"pogue", "errormix", "Blends the wet sound towards the rounding error itself, a hiss that follows the sound."},
  {"pogue", "lpcut", "Low-pass on the wet sound, in Hz. Darkens the chorus and the feedback."},
  {"pogue", "lpq", "Resonance of the low-pass."},
  {"pogue", "hpcut", "High-pass on the wet sound, in Hz. Keeps the bass out of the chorus and the feedback."},
  {"pogue", "hpq", "Resonance of the high-pass."},
  {"pogue", "inertia", "How slowly each voice glides to a new delay, in ms. 0 jumps, which with a low resolution clicks."},
  {"pogue", "postgain", "Output level, in dB."},
  {"pogue", "mix", "Amount of the wet sound added to the dry sound."},
  {"pogue", "onset", "What moves the voices. 0: the random timer. Above 0: a new random target is picked when the sound has a hit or a sudden change of pitch, and the timer stops. The value is the sensitivity: low only catches hard hits, high catches soft ones and small steps in pitch too. Change is still how far the voices go."},
  {"pogue", "pattern", "A row of steps that move the voices on the song's grid; 0 is off. Click the steps in the panel. The first step is the highest bit, so the row reads as a binary number from the left. Works together with onset, and also stops the random timer."},
  {"pogue", "patlen", "How many steps the pattern has (1 to 16)."},
  {"pogue", "patrate", "How long a pattern step lasts, in song ticks (the arp's list: 1 is a tick, 4 a beat at 4 ticks to the beat)."},
  {"pogue", "moves", "What a trigger does. New targets: each voice picks a new random delay to glide to (needs Change above 0 to be heard). Phase jump: each voice's slow sine jumps to a random place. Both does both."},
  {"pogue", "lookahead", "Live: the sound is not delayed, and the move follows a hit by a fraction of a ms and a step in pitch by a few periods. Aligned: the sound is held back 10 ms so the move lands on the hit itself; the node reports that latency (only when onset is above 0)."},
  {"phaser", "rate", "Speed of the sweep in Hz."},
  {"phaser", "depth", "How far the sweep goes either side of `freq`, in octaves."},
  {"phaser", "freq", "Centre frequency of the sweep in Hz."},
  {"phaser", "stages", "Number of allpass stages. Every two make one notch, so more stages are a deeper, richer phase."},
  {"phaser", "feedback", "Sends the output back for a sharper, more vocal sweep. Negative values give a hollow, inverted sound."},
  {"phaser", "width", "Offsets the right channel's sweep from the left for stereo width."},
  {"phaser", "mix", "Amount of the phased sound added to the dry sound. 1 gives the deepest notches."},
  {"phaser", "phase", "Where in its cycle the sweep restarts, 0 to 1. Nothing happens until a pattern writes it (expose it as a column): then the sweep slides to this point and runs on from there, so it can be lined up with the beat."},
  {"phaser", "glide", "Milliseconds the sweep takes to slide to a phase written by a pattern. 0 jumps at once, which clicks unless the sound is silent (set it as a note starts)."},
  {"flanger", "rate", "Speed of the sweep in Hz."},
  {"flanger", "depth", "How far the delay swings, in ms either side of `delay`."},
  {"flanger", "delay", "Centre of the sweep in ms. Shorter is a higher, thinner comb."},
  {"flanger", "feedback", "Sends the delayed sound back in for a harder, more metallic effect. Negative inverts it."},
  {"flanger", "width", "Offsets the right channel's sweep from the left for stereo width."},
  {"flanger", "mix", "Amount of flanged sound added to the dry sound. 1 gives the deepest comb."},
  {"flanger", "phase", "Where in its cycle the sweep restarts, 0 to 1. Nothing happens until a pattern writes it (expose it as a column): then the sweep slides to this point and runs on from there, so it can be lined up with the beat."},
  {"flanger", "glide", "Milliseconds the sweep takes to slide to a phase written by a pattern. 0 jumps at once, which clicks unless the sound is silent (set it as a note starts)."},
  {"distortion", "drive", "How hard the signal is pushed into the clipper. More is louder and more distorted."},
  {"distortion", "tone", "Lowpass frequency in Hz after the clipper, which takes the fizz off. Lower is darker."},
  {"distortion", "mode", "0 soft (tanh), 1 hard clip, 2 tube (asymmetric, adds even harmonics), 3 fuzz (hard, squared off)."},
  {"distortion", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. 1 is all distorted."},
  {"distortion", "level", "Output level after the distortion."},
  {"distortion", "oversample", "Runs the clipper at 1x, 2x or 4x (0, 1, 2) to reduce aliasing. Higher is cleaner and costs more CPU."},
  {"multitap", "time", "Time in ms between taps."},
  {"multitap", "taps", "How many echoes (taps) are produced."},
  {"multitap", "decay", "Level of each tap relative to the one before it. 1 keeps them all equal."},
  {"multitap", "spread", "How far the taps are panned from left to right across the sweep. 0 is centred."},
  {"multitap", "feedback", "How much of the last tap goes back in, for repeating patterns."},
  {"multitap", "mix", "Amount of the echoes added to the dry sound."},
  {"shiva", "length", "Time in ms at the right edge of the tap view. All taps stretch with it, so it also works as a master delay time."},
  {"shiva", "feedback", "How much of the latest tap goes back in. The whole pattern then repeats each time it ends."},
  {"shiva", "damp", "Lowpass in the feedback path: higher makes each repeat darker."},
  {"shiva", "mix", "Amount of the echoes added to the dry sound."},
  {"blur", "freq", "Blurs the spectrum across frequency: the fine detail of the harmonics melts into a breathy wash. 0 leaves it sharp."},
  {"blur", "time", "Blurs across time, in ms: each frequency follows a slow average, so attacks smear and sounds bloom. 0 is off."},
  {"blur", "freeze", "Holds the blur where it is, so the sound hangs in the air until you let go."},
  {"blur", "phase", "0 keeps the sound's own phases (pitch and articulation survive); 1 uses random phases for a smooth, pad-like smear."},
  {"blur", "size", "Frame length: 1024, 2048 or 4096 samples. Longer is smoother and adds more delay."},
  {"blur", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. The dry sound is delayed to match, so mixing stays in time."},
  {"saturator", "drive", "How hard the sound is pushed into the curve, in dB. The level is kept about the same."},
  {"saturator", "type", "The curve: soft (tanh), tape (rounder, gentler), tube (lopsided: adds even harmonics, warmer) or clip (clean to the knee, then a firm wall)."},
  {"saturator", "bias", "Leans the sound off-centre into the curve, which adds even harmonics to any type. 0 is centred."},
  {"saturator", "bass", "Everything below this frequency stays out of the curve, so bass and kick stay clean. 20 Hz is off."},
  {"saturator", "tone", "A lowpass after the curve, to take off the top it makes. 20 kHz is off."},
  {"saturator", "mix", "Dry/wet balance, for parallel saturation. The dry sound is delayed to match."},
  {"saturator", "out", "Level at the end, in dB."},
  {"saturator", "quality", "standard runs the curve at 2x oversampling, eco at 1x (cheaper, a little more aliasing), hq at 4x."},
  {"mbsat", "f1", "First crossover, in Hz: below it is the low band."},
  {"mbsat", "f2", "Second crossover, in Hz."},
  {"mbsat", "f3", "Third crossover, in Hz: above it is the high band."},
  {"mbsat", "type", "The curve for all four bands: soft, tape, tube or clip."},
  {"mbsat", "bias", "Leans the sound into the curve for even harmonics, in every band."},
  {"mbsat", "solo", "Hear one band alone (1 to 4), or all (0)."},
  {"mbsat", "mix", "Dry/wet balance, for parallel saturation."},
  {"mbsat", "out", "Level at the end, in dB."},
  {"mbsat", "quality", "standard runs the curves at 2x oversampling, eco at 1x (cheaper), hq at 4x."},
  {"mbsat", "lowDrive", "Drive of the low band, in dB."},
  {"mbsat", "lowGain", "Level of the low band after the curve, in dB."},
  {"mbsat", "lowmidDrive", "Drive of the low mid band, in dB."},
  {"mbsat", "lowmidGain", "Level of the low mid band after the curve, in dB."},
  {"mbsat", "highmidDrive", "Drive of the high mid band, in dB."},
  {"mbsat", "highmidGain", "Level of the high mid band after the curve, in dB."},
  {"mbsat", "highDrive", "Drive of the high band, in dB."},
  {"mbsat", "highGain", "Level of the high band after the curve, in dB."},
  {"hpss", "length", "How long, in ms, a held tone has to last to count as harmonic. Longer keeps only steady notes in harm."},
  {"hpss", "width", "How wide, in Hz, a hit has to be across the spectrum to count as percussive. Wider keeps only broad noisy hits in perc."},
  {"hpss", "sharp", "How firmly each part of the sound is given to one output or the other. 1 shares it softly, 8 is nearly a switch."},
  {"hpss", "size", "Frame length: 1024, 2048 or 4096 samples. Longer separates tones better and adds more delay."},
  {"bodyblur", "smooth", "Blurs the body of the sound across frequency: the gritty detail between its harmonics melts away. Hits are not touched."},
  {"bodyblur", "time", "In ms. How slowly the body follows the sound: longer rounds off the stair-steps of a crushed sound and lets it bloom."},
  {"bodyblur", "round", "A gentle high cut on the body only, for the fizz and aliasing that crushing leaves at the top. Hits keep their brightness."},
  {"bodyblur", "split", "How firmly body and hits are told apart. 1 shares them softly, 8 is nearly a switch."},
  {"bodyblur", "size", "Frame length: 1024, 2048 or 4096 samples. Longer separates tones better and adds more delay."},
  {"bodyblur", "quality", "Eco works out the body/hit split every other frame, for about half the CPU. Standard is the full split."},
  {"bodyblur", "mix", "Dry against smoothed. The dry sound is delayed to match, so it stays in time."},
  {"specdyn", "threshold", "In dB below full scale. A frequency louder than this is turned down."},
  {"specdyn", "ratio", "How hard: 4 means 4 dB over the threshold comes out 1 dB over. 20 is nearly a limiter."},
  {"specdyn", "tilt", "Leans the whole spectrum, in dB for each octave about 1 kHz: positive is brighter, negative darker."},
  {"specdyn", "attack", "How quickly, in ms, a frequency is turned down when it gets loud."},
  {"specdyn", "release", "How quickly, in ms, it comes back when it gets quiet."},
  {"specdyn", "smooth", "Averages the gain over neighbouring frequencies, so it does not whistle. 0 treats each one alone."},
  {"specdyn", "makeup", "Gain added after the compression, in dB."},
  {"specdyn", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. The dry sound is delayed to match, so mixing stays in time."},
  {"specdyn", "size", "Frame length: 1024, 2048 or 4096 samples. Longer is smoother and adds more delay."},
  {"islands", "contrast", "Sharpens first: the quiet part of the spectrum falls away and the blobs get edges. 0 leaves the sound as it is."},
  {"islands", "islands", "How far everything off a peak is turned down. Each loud blob keeps a thin ridge at its peak; 1 turns the rest down by the full depth."},
  {"islands", "depth", "The most the sound between the peaks is turned down, in dB."},
  {"islands", "smooth", "How slowly each frequency's gain changes from one frame to the next. Low sounds watery and flickers; high is smooth but slower to follow a changing note."},
  {"islands", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. The dry sound is delayed to match, so mixing stays in time."},
  {"islands", "size", "Frame length: 2048 to 16384 samples. Longer tells close notes apart and low notes better, and adds a lot of delay (16384 is about a third of a second)."},
  {"mosh", "motion", "How much of the modulator's change in loudness is added to the held picture, frequency by frequency. 1 is dB for dB, 2 is double."},
  {"mosh", "slide", "How much of the modulator's sideways motion (a voice gliding up, a sweep) drags the held picture along with it. 0 only brightens and darkens."},
  {"mosh", "keyframe", "In ms. How often the picture is replaced by the live sound again, a clean frame before the smear grows. 0 means only the first one."},
  {"mosh", "hold", "How much of the input's own movement is thrown away between keyframes. 1 freezes the picture so only the modulator moves it; 0 lets the input keep playing."},
  {"mosh", "heal", "How quickly the picture drifts back toward the live sound between keyframes. 0 never does."},
  {"mosh", "bloom", "How far new energy can grow at frequencies that were empty at the keyframe. 0 grows none; 1 lets ghost partials come up to the level of the loudest."},
  {"mosh", "smooth", "Blurs the modulator's spectrum before it is measured, so single noisy frequencies do not move things."},
  {"mosh", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. The dry sound is delayed to match, so mixing stays in time."},
  {"mosh", "size", "Frame length: 1024, 2048 or 4096 samples. Longer is smoother and adds more delay."},
  {"xsynth", "amount", "How much of the modulator's spectral shape is used. 0 leaves the sound alone, 1 is all of it."},
  {"xsynth", "smooth", "How broad a shape is taken. Low keeps the modulator's fine detail, high only its broad colour."},
  {"xsynth", "detail", "Also lends the modulator's own harmonics, so a pitched voice makes the noise pitched."},
  {"xsynth", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. The dry sound is delayed to match, so mixing stays in time."},
  {"xsynth", "size", "Frame length: 1024, 2048 or 4096 samples. Longer is smoother and adds more delay."},
  {"gater", "div", "Ticks per step, snapped to 1/4, 1/2, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 or 8. With 4 ticks to the beat, 1 gives sixteenths and 2 eighths; with 8 ticks to the beat, 2 gives sixteenths. 1/2 and 1/4 are half and quarter ticks, for rolls and stutters."},
  {"gater", "steps", "How many steps (up to 64) are used before the pattern starts again. Steps you have not drawn repeat the ones before, so raise this to make a longer phrase and then edit it."},
  {"gater", "shift", "Rotates the pattern by this many steps, so a command can slide the rhythm along."},
  {"gater", "depth", "How far the gate closes. 1 is silence on the closed steps; less lets some sound through."},
  {"gater", "smooth", "Time in ms the gate takes to open and close: short is choppy, long is a soft swell."},
  {"freqshift", "shift", "Moves every frequency by this many Hz, up or down. Small values give a slow shimmering detune, large ones bells and robot voices."},
  {"freqshift", "feedback", "Sends the shifted sound back in, so each pass climbs another step: rising or falling spirals."},
  {"freqshift", "mix", "Dry/wet balance."},
  {"specpan", "shape", "The pattern across the spectrum: 0 alternate (neighbouring bands to opposite sides, the classic widener), 1 sweep (lows left to highs right), 2 wave (two smooth swings), 3 custom (the b1..b10 sliders, low band to high)."},
  {"specpan", "spread", "How far the bands move toward their pan position. 0 leaves the sound untouched; around 0.5 is a subtle widening, 1 is the full pattern."},
  {"specpan", "rotate", "Slides the pattern along the bands. Modulate or write it from a pattern and the image drifts."},
  {"specpan", "lowMono", "Everything below this frequency is made mono, which keeps kick and bass solid in the middle and safe on any speaker system."},
  {"specpan", "mix", "Dry/wet balance."},
  {"specpan", "b1", "Pan position of this band (b1 lowest, b10 highest), used by the custom shape: -1 left, 0 where it is, 1 right."},
  {"specpan", "rate", "Speed of the built-in motion in Hz: it moves the rotate position back and forth (or round) on its own. 0 holds the image still, and wired or written rotate still works on top."},
  {"specpan", "sweep", "How far the built-in motion carries rotate. Small values sway the image, large ones sweep it across the whole spectrum."},
  {"specpan", "wave", "The motion's shape: sine and triangle sway, saw is a steady drift round the bands, square jumps between two positions."},
  {"specpan", "punch", "Keeps attacks tight: while a hit goes by (a kick, a snare) the stereo image is pulled back to the centre, then opens again. Without it the click of a sweeping kick can split left and right and sound crunchy. 0 is off."},
  {"specpan", "punchTime", "How long, in ms, the image takes to open again after an attack. Short keeps only the click in the middle, long keeps the whole body of the hit centred."},
  {"waveshaper", "drive", "Scales the signal before it meets the curve you drew: more drive uses more of the curve's ends."},
  {"waveshaper", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. 1 is all shaped."},
  {"waveshaper", "oversample", "Runs the shaper at 1x, 2x or 4x (0, 1, 2) to reduce aliasing."},
  {"waveshaper", "level", "Output level after shaping."},
  {"wavefolder", "fold", "How hard the wave is pushed into the folder. Each overshoot turns the wave round, so more fold means more harmonics."},
  {"wavefolder", "symmetry", "Moves the wave off centre before folding: uneven folds, which add even harmonics."},
  {"wavefolder", "drive", "Input gain before the folder."},
  {"wavefolder", "mix", "Dry/wet balance."},
  {"wavefolder", "oversample", "Runs the folder at 1x, 2x or 4x (0, 1, 2) to keep the new harmonics from aliasing."},
  {"wavefolder", "level", "Output level."},
  {"pluckgate", "mode", "0 opens gain and low-pass filter together (the classic pluck), 1 only the gain, 2 only the filter."},
  {"pluckgate", "bright", "The highest cutoff the gate opens to."},
  {"pluckgate", "res", "Resonance of the gate's filter."},
  {"pluckgate", "track", "How far the open cutoff follows the note's pitch."},
  {"pluckgate", "open", "How quickly the vactrol opens, in ms."},
  {"pluckgate", "close", "How slowly the vactrol lets go, in ms. This is the length of the ring-out."},
  {"pluckgate", "atk", "Strike envelope attack (used when nothing is plugged into cv)."},
  {"pluckgate", "dec", "Strike envelope decay."},
  {"pluckgate", "sus", "Strike envelope sustain."},
  {"pluckgate", "rel", "Strike envelope release."},
  {"pluckgate", "vel", "How much the note's velocity sets the strike."},
  {"pluckgate", "level", "Output level."},
  {"westcoast", "wave", "The principal oscillator: sine, triangle, saw, square, or the wave drawn in the panel."},
  {"westcoast", "tune", "Pitch offset, in semitones."},
  {"westcoast", "ratio", "Pitch of the modulation oscillator against the principal's."},
  {"westcoast", "modtune", "Fine tuning of the modulation oscillator, in cents."},
  {"westcoast", "modwave", "Shape of the modulation oscillator: sine or triangle."},
  {"westcoast", "fm", "How far the modulation oscillator bends the principal's phase: soft to metallic."},
  {"westcoast", "sync", "On: the principal restarts every cycle of the modulation oscillator."},
  {"westcoast", "fold", "How hard the wave is pushed into the wave folder."},
  {"westcoast", "symmetry", "Moves the wave off centre before folding (adds even harmonics)."},
  {"westcoast", "foldenv", "How much the timbre envelope adds to fold."},
  {"westcoast", "fmenv", "How much the timbre envelope adds to fm."},
  {"westcoast", "tatk", "Timbre envelope attack, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "tdec", "Timbre envelope decay, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "tsus", "Timbre envelope sustain."},
  {"westcoast", "trel", "Timbre envelope release, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "mode", "Pluck gate: 0 gain and filter together, 1 gain only, 2 filter only."},
  {"westcoast", "bright", "The highest cutoff of the pluck gate."},
  {"westcoast", "res", "Resonance of the pluck gate's filter."},
  {"westcoast", "track", "How far the cutoff follows the note's pitch."},
  {"westcoast", "lpgatk", "How quickly the vactrol opens, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "lpgdec", "How slowly the vactrol lets go, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "atk", "Strike envelope attack, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "dec", "Strike envelope decay, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "sus", "Strike envelope sustain."},
  {"westcoast", "rel", "Strike envelope release, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "glide", "Slide time between notes, ms."},
  {"westcoast", "vel", "How much the note's velocity sets the strike."},
  {"westcoast", "level", "Output level."},
  {"westcoast", "quality", "High runs the oscillator and folder at twice the rate, ultra at four times (the least aliasing)."},
  {"westcoast", "drift", "How much the fold, fm and pitch wander, and how much each note differs from the last."},
  {"random", "mode", "Free running (a new value `rate` times a second), a new value on each note, or a new value each time the trig input fires."},
  {"random", "rate", "How often a new value is picked in free running mode, in Hz."},
  {"random", "smooth", "How far it slides to each new value: 0 jumps (sample and hold), 1 takes the whole time."},
  {"random", "amount", "Size of the values."},
  {"random", "bipolar", "1: values between minus and plus amount. 0: between 0 and amount."},
  {"random", "low", "Bottom of the range the picks fall in, before bipolar and amount. Set bipolar to 0 to read it as the output itself."},
  {"random", "high", "Top of the range the picks fall in."},
  {"random", "lean", "The value inside the range that picks favour."},
  {"random", "focus", "How strongly picks crowd around lean: 0 is even across the range, 1 mostly near lean with the odd one reaching the ends."},
  {"drumsynth2", "startPitch", "Pitch in Hz at the top of the pitch drawing, at the root note: where the hit begins."},
  {"drumsynth2", "endPitch", "Pitch in Hz at the bottom of the pitch drawing. The pitch moves between the two evenly in octaves, so a straight line is a smooth glide."},
  {"drumsynth2", "pitchMul", "Multiplies both pitches: 2 is an octave up."},
  {"drumsynth2", "keytrack", "On: the note moves the pitches (the root note plays them as written)."},
  {"drumsynth2", "root", "The note that plays the pitches as written."},
  {"drumsynth2", "prate", "How fast the pitch drawing plays. 1 takes 250 ms; 2 takes half that, 0.5 twice as long."},
  {"drumsynth2", "body", "Level of the pitched part."},
  {"drumsynth2", "arate", "How fast the volume drawing plays. 1 takes 700 ms; lower is a longer tail."},
  {"drumsynth2", "harm", "Harmonics added to the body, strongest at the start of the volume drawing and fading to a clean sine in the tail. This is the thump you can hear on a small speaker, which cannot play the low note itself."},
  {"drumsynth2", "drive", "Soft clipping of the whole hit before the boost."},
  {"drumsynth2", "noise", "How much noise."},
  {"drumsynth2", "noiseType", "White, pink, brown, blue, violet, velvet (sparse spikes), crackle (pops of random size) or stepped (held values; `noiseTone` sets how often it steps)."},
  {"drumsynth2", "noiseTone", "Low-pass on the noise: takes the top off. For stepped noise, how often it steps."},
  {"drumsynth2", "noiseHigh", "High-pass on the noise: takes the bottom off."},
  {"drumsynth2", "nrate", "How fast the noise drawing plays. 1 takes 400 ms."},
  {"drumsynth2", "click", "The short burst at the very start of the hit, as in drum synth."},
  {"drumsynth2", "clickMode", "Synthesized (a damped sine) or the start of a sample from the node's list."},
  {"drumsynth2", "clickLen", "Length of the click, in ms."},
  {"drumsynth2", "clickTone", "Pitch of the synthesized click."},
  {"drumsynth2", "clickSample", "Which sample of the node's list is the click."},
  {"drumsynth2", "clickTail", "Milliseconds of smooth fade at the end of a sample click."},
  {"drumsynth2", "boost", "Low shelf at the very end of the chain, in dB: weight under the hit. A soft limiter follows, so it cannot clip."},
  {"drumsynth2", "boostFreq", "Where the boost sits, in Hz: everything below it is lifted."},
  {"drumsynth2", "vel", "How much the note's velocity sets the level."},
  {"drumsynth2", "level", "Output level."},
  {"env2", "time", "How long the drawing takes from its first anchor to its last, in ms. After that it holds its last level."},
  {"env2", "amount", "Size of the output: the drawing (0 to 1) times this."},
  {"env2", "loop", "On: the drawing starts again at its end, so it repeats like a wave of your own."},
  {"env2", "vel", "How much the note's velocity scales the output."},
  {"dcblock", "freq", "Cutoff of the high pass that removes the offset, in Hz. Lower keeps more bass but settles more slowly."},
  {"exciter", "freq", "Where the excited band starts, in Hz. Only the sound above it is processed."},
  {"exciter", "drive", "How hard that band is saturated."},
  {"exciter", "amount", "How much of the processed band is added back to the original."},
  {"exciter", "even", "Biases the saturator so it adds even harmonics (warmer, more tube-like)."},
  {"transient", "attack", "Boosts (+) or cuts (-) the attacking part of each sound, by up to 12 dB."},
  {"transient", "sustain", "Boosts (+) or cuts (-) the sustaining tail of each sound, by up to 12 dB."},
  {"transient", "speed", "Scales the time of the detectors. Lower is slower and treats longer events as the attack."},
  {"split", "f1", "Crossover between the low band and the low-mid band, in Hz."},
  {"split", "f2", "Crossover between the low-mid band and the high-mid band, in Hz."},
  {"split", "f3", "Crossover between the high-mid band and the high band, in Hz. The crossovers are kept in order."},
  {"compressor", "threshold", "Level in dB above which the sound is turned down."},
  {"compressor", "ratio", "How much louder than the threshold the input must get for 1 dB more output. 4 means 4 dB in gives 1 dB out."},
  {"compressor", "attack", "How fast the compressor reacts when the level rises, in ms. Slow lets the start of a note through."},
  {"compressor", "release", "How fast it lets go after the level falls, in ms."},
  {"compressor", "knee", "Softness of the bend at the threshold, in dB. 0 is hard, larger eases in gently."},
  {"compressor", "makeup", "Gain added after compression, in dB, to bring the level back up."},
  {"compressor", "mix", "Blend with the dry signal, for parallel compression."},
  {"gate", "threshold", "Level in dB above which the gate opens."},
  {"gate", "hyst", "How far below the threshold (dB) the level must fall before the gate may close, so it does not chatter."},
  {"gate", "attack", "How fast the gate opens, in ms."},
  {"gate", "hold", "How long the gate stays open after the level falls below the close point, in ms."},
  {"gate", "release", "How fast the gate closes, in ms."},
  {"gate", "range", "Level in dB left when the gate is closed. -80 is silence; 0 does nothing."},
  {"envfx", "trig", "The trigger: any value above 0 starts the envelope, 0 releases it, and writing another non-zero value while it is on restarts the attack. Expose it as a pattern column, or wire notetrig's `trig` into its port."},
  {"envfx", "attack", "Attack in ms: how fast the sound comes up after a trigger."},
  {"envfx", "decay", "Decay in ms: how long it takes to fall to the sustain level."},
  {"envfx", "sustain", "Level held while triggered, from 0 to 1. At 0 it is a percussive hit that dies after the decay."},
  {"envfx", "release", "Release in ms after the trigger is released."},
  {"envfx", "range", "Level in dB when the envelope is at 0. -80 is silent; 0 lets everything through whatever the envelope does."},
  {"eq", "lowcut", "High-pass frequency in Hz (12 dB per octave). At 20 Hz it is off."},
  {"eq", "highcut", "Low-pass frequency in Hz (12 dB per octave). At 20000 Hz it is off."},
  {"eq", "f1", "Band 1 (low shelf) frequency in Hz."},
  {"eq", "g1", "Band 1 gain in dB. 0 turns the band off."},
  {"eq", "q1", "Band 1 slope. Lower is gentler."},
  {"eq", "f2", "Band 2 (peak) centre frequency in Hz."},
  {"eq", "g2", "Band 2 gain in dB. 0 turns the band off."},
  {"eq", "q2", "Band 2 width. Higher is narrower."},
  {"eq", "f3", "Band 3 (peak) centre frequency in Hz."},
  {"eq", "g3", "Band 3 gain in dB. 0 turns the band off."},
  {"eq", "q3", "Band 3 width. Higher is narrower."},
  {"eq", "f4", "Band 4 (peak) centre frequency in Hz."},
  {"eq", "g4", "Band 4 gain in dB. 0 turns the band off."},
  {"eq", "q4", "Band 4 width. Higher is narrower."},
  {"eq", "f5", "Band 5 (high shelf) frequency in Hz."},
  {"eq", "g5", "Band 5 gain in dB. 0 turns the band off."},
  {"eq", "q5", "Band 5 slope. Lower is gentler."},
  {"crusher", "bits", "Bit depth. Lower is rougher; it may be fractional (4.5 is between 4 and 5 bits)."},
  {"crusher", "rate", "Sample rate in Hz the sound is held at. Lower is more aliased and gritty. At or above the engine's rate there is no reduction."},
  {"crusher", "jitter", "Randomises when each sample is taken. 0 is steady, 1 is a rough, gritty rate."},
  {"crusher", "dither", "Adds a little noise before rounding so quiet passages crackle instead of going stepped and silent."},
  {"crusher", "mix", "Blend with the clean signal."},
  {"tape", "type", "The kind of tape and machine. studio reel: clean and fast. chrome cassette: bright. ferric cassette: an ordinary cassette, duller and warm. worn cassette: dark, wobbling, hissy, with dropouts. dictaphone: only the middle of the sound, a lot of wobble. dubbed copy: a copy of a copy, dark and saturated."},
  {"tape", "age", "How worn the tape is. Older is darker, wobbles more, hisses more and drops out more."},
  {"tape", "drive", "How hard the tape is driven. It squashes the loud parts and adds warm harmonics."},
  {"tape", "wow", "Slow pitch drift, about once a second. Scaled by the type and the age."},
  {"tape", "flutter", "Fast pitch shiver, 7 to 12 times a second. Scaled by the type and the age."},
  {"tape", "hiss", "The hiss of the tape. Its level depends on the type and the age."},
  {"tape", "dropouts", "How often the tape drops out: short dips in level, dulling the sound as they go. Rarer on a young tape."},
  {"tape", "tone", "Moves the loss of highs: left is duller, right is brighter."},
  {"tape", "mix", "Blend with the clean sound (delayed by about 4.5 ms to match)."},
  {"mbcomp", "f1", "Crossover between the low and the low-mid band, in Hz."},
  {"mbcomp", "f2", "Crossover between the low-mid and the high-mid band, in Hz."},
  {"mbcomp", "f3", "Crossover between the high-mid and the high band, in Hz. The crossovers are kept in order."},
  {"mbcomp", "knee", "Softness of the bend at the threshold, in dB, the same for every band."},
  {"mbcomp", "solo", "Listen to one band alone to set it up: 1 low, 2 low mid, 3 high mid, 4 high. 0 plays them all."},
  {"mbcomp", "mix", "Blend with the dry sound, for parallel compression."},
  {"mbcomp", "out", "Gain at the end, in dB."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowThresh", "Level in dB above which the low band is turned down."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowRatio", "How much louder than the threshold the low band must get for 1 dB more output."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowAttack", "How fast the low band reacts when its level rises, in ms."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowRelease", "How fast the low band lets go after its level falls, in ms."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowGain", "Gain added to the low band after compression, in dB."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowmidThresh", "Level in dB above which the low-mid band is turned down."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowmidRatio", "How much louder than the threshold the low-mid band must get for 1 dB more output."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowmidAttack", "How fast the low-mid band reacts when its level rises, in ms."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowmidRelease", "How fast the low-mid band lets go after its level falls, in ms."},
  {"mbcomp", "lowmidGain", "Gain added to the low-mid band after compression, in dB."},
  {"mbcomp", "highmidThresh", "Level in dB above which the high-mid band is turned down."},
  {"mbcomp", "highmidRatio", "How much louder than the threshold the high-mid band must get for 1 dB more output."},
  {"mbcomp", "highmidAttack", "How fast the high-mid band reacts when its level rises, in ms."},
  {"mbcomp", "highmidRelease", "How fast the high-mid band lets go after its level falls, in ms."},
  {"mbcomp", "highmidGain", "Gain added to the high-mid band after compression, in dB."},
  {"mbcomp", "highThresh", "Level in dB above which the high band is turned down."},
  {"mbcomp", "highRatio", "How much louder than the threshold the high band must get for 1 dB more output."},
  {"mbcomp", "highAttack", "How fast the high band reacts when its level rises, in ms."},
  {"mbcomp", "highRelease", "How fast the high band lets go after its level falls, in ms."},
  {"mbcomp", "highGain", "Gain added to the high band after compression, in dB."},
  {"prismacanyon", "time", "The repeat time of the first delay line, in ms; the others sit at 2, 3 and 4 times it, so every echo falls on a multiple of it. Set it from the tick lines on the slider and the echoes land on the grid. Moving it while the sound plays bends the pitch of the echoes, as turning the repeat rate of a tape echo does."},
  {"prismacanyon", "intensity", "How much of the delay lines goes back to the tape. Past 1 it feeds on itself and the echoes build up; the saturation holds it in check."},
  {"prismacanyon", "cutoff", "The brightest a delay line gets, in Hz. Each line is darkened from here by its own damp."},
  {"prismacanyon", "bass", "Bass of the sound that goes round the loop, in dB. Each repeat gets a little more of it, or less."},
  {"prismacanyon", "treble", "Treble of the sound that goes round the loop, in dB. Each repeat gets a little more of it, or less."},
  {"prismacanyon", "echo", "The volume of the echoes."},
  {"prismacanyon", "reverb", "The volume of a small spring-like reverb of the input."},
  {"prismacanyon", "dry", "The volume of the input itself."},
  {"prismacanyon", "wow", "The slow drift of the tape speed, a pitch wobble about once a second and a half."},
  {"prismacanyon", "flutter", "The fast shiver of the tape speed."},
  {"prismacanyon", "saturation", "How hard the tape is driven: it squashes the loud parts and rounds the repeats."},
  {"prismacanyon", "level1", "Level of delay line 1, at 1 times the repeat time. 0 switches the line off, in the echoes and in the loop."},
  {"prismacanyon", "pan1", "Where delay line 1 sits in the stereo field: -1 left, 1 right."},
  {"prismacanyon", "damp1", "How much delay line 1 is darkened: its low-pass sits at the cutoff, lowered by up to four octaves (1) for the far lines."},
  {"prismacanyon", "level2", "Level of delay line 2, at 2 times the repeat time. 0 switches the line off, in the echoes and in the loop."},
  {"prismacanyon", "pan2", "Where delay line 2 sits in the stereo field: -1 left, 1 right."},
  {"prismacanyon", "damp2", "How much delay line 2 is darkened: its low-pass sits at the cutoff, lowered by up to four octaves (1) for the far lines."},
  {"prismacanyon", "level3", "Level of delay line 3, at 3 times the repeat time. 0 switches the line off, in the echoes and in the loop."},
  {"prismacanyon", "pan3", "Where delay line 3 sits in the stereo field: -1 left, 1 right."},
  {"prismacanyon", "damp3", "How much delay line 3 is darkened: its low-pass sits at the cutoff, lowered by up to four octaves (1) for the far lines."},
  {"prismacanyon", "level4", "Level of delay line 4, at 4 times the repeat time. 0 switches the line off, in the echoes and in the loop."},
  {"prismacanyon", "pan4", "Where delay line 4 sits in the stereo field: -1 left, 1 right."},
  {"prismacanyon", "damp4", "How much delay line 4 is darkened: its low-pass sits at the cutoff, lowered by up to four octaves (1) for the far lines."},
  {"ctrl", "to", "Write a value and the output glides to it from where it is now over `time`. Writing the same value again glides again."},
  {"ctrl", "from", "Write a value and the output jumps there at once. The next `to` starts from it."},
  {"ctrl", "time", "Length of the glide in subticks (16 subticks are one tick), 0 to 255. 0 is a jump. Read when `to` is written."},
  {"ctrl", "curve", "Shape of the glide: 0 linear, 1 log, 2 fast in slow out, 3 slow in fast out, 4 random stagger."},
  {"ctrl", "seed", "Seed for the random stagger: the same seed gives the same stagger every time."},
  {"ctrl", "steps", "For the random stagger: how many random steps the value takes to arrive."},
  {"ctrl", "wobble", "For the random stagger: how far the steps may stray from the straight path."},
  {"notetrig", "legato", "1: a note played while another is still held does not start the envelope again. 0: every note fires it."},
  {"arp", "mode", "The order the held notes are played in. up, down, up/down, down/up (the ends play once); up/down+ and down/up+ (the ends play twice); converge (lowest, highest, next lowest, next highest...); diverge (the same, backwards: from the middle out); converge/diverge; as played (the order the keys went down); random (a free pick each step); random other (every note once in a random order, then again); random once (one random order, kept until the keys change); chord (all notes at once on every step); repeat x2, repeat x4 (each note twice or four times, ascending); pinky up and thumb up (the highest, or the lowest, note between every other note), and their up/down versions; interval cycle (each held note, then +cycle1, then +cycle2: the tracker 0xy arpeggio)."},
  {"arp", "rate", "How long a step is, in ticks. 1 tick is a sixteenth with 4 ticks to the beat; 1/2 tick is a thirty-second; 2/3 and 1/3 give triplet-like grids; 4 ticks is a quarter note."},
  {"arp", "gate", "How long each note is held, as a fraction of the step. Short is staccato, 100% fills the step, above 100% the notes overlap (legato)."},
  {"arp", "swing", "Delays every second step, as the song's swing does. 50 is straight, 66.7 is triplet swing, 75 is a hard dotted shuffle."},
  {"arp", "octaves", "How many octaves the held notes are repeated over (1 is just the notes you play). The mode then orders the whole set, so up with 2 octaves climbs through both."},
  {"arp", "transpose", "Moves everything the arp plays by this many semitones."},
  {"arp", "steps", "How many of the 16 mask steps are used before the mask starts again. 16 is a bar of sixteenths; 12 or 10 make polyrhythms against the pattern."},
  {"arp", "hold", "1: the arp keeps going after the keys are let go. The next key pressed after that starts a new chord. Turn hold off to stop it."},
  {"arp", "retrigger", "When the pattern goes back to its first note: never (it runs on and only follows the grid), with every new key, or every 4, 8 or 16 steps. Every new key is the usual choice for a played arp; every 16 steps keeps a long pattern in step with the bar."},
  {"arp", "offset", "Starts the pattern this many steps in, each time it restarts. 1 plays the second note first."},
  {"arp", "repeats", "How many times the pattern plays before the arp falls silent, until the next restart. 0 plays for ever. 1 or 2 with a short gate make a strum."},
  {"arp", "seed", "Starts the random modes. The same seed always gives the same notes, and the pattern restarts from it, so a random arp repeats exactly."},
  {"arp", "veltype", "played: each step has the velocity the keys were played with. fixed: the level parameter sets it."},
  {"arp", "level", "The loudness of the arp: the velocity when it is fixed, otherwise a scale on the played velocity. The height of each mask bar is a further scale."},
  {"arp", "humanize", "Random variation of the velocity from step to step. 0 is exact."},
  {"arp", "cycle1", "Interval cycle mode: the second note of each triple, in semitones above the held note (4 is a major third)."},
  {"arp", "cycle2", "Interval cycle mode: the third note of each triple, in semitones above the held note (7 is a fifth)."},
  {"arp", "thru", "1: the keys you play sound as well as the arpeggio, handy for a bass note under the arp."},
  {"chord", "chord", "Which of the 256 chord slots (hex 00 to FF) plays. Put it in a pattern with the Chd column. A slot with no name plays the note as it is."},
  {"chord", "octave", "The octave the chord is built in: its root sits here (4 is the octave of middle C). The Oct column writes it, one hex digit; 2 to 9 is the useful range."},
  {"chord", "inversion", "Moves the lowest notes up an octave, one at a time: 1 is the first inversion, 2 the second. Lifts the whole chord a little each time without changing the harmony."},
  {"chord", "notes", "The most notes the chord may have, for a synth with few voices. The fifth is dropped first (the way a pianist leaves it out), then the highest notes."},
  {"chord", "strum", "Time between one note of the chord and the next, in ms. 0 plays them together; 20 to 40 is a guitar-like strum."},
  {"chord", "direction", "Which end of the chord the strum starts from: the lowest note (a down-strum) or the highest (an up-strum)."},
  {"chord", "follow", "0: the chord is played as typed, whatever note is pressed; the note only triggers it. 1: the chord moves with the note, so C4 plays it as typed and D4 a tone higher."},
  {"chord", "level", "Scales the velocity of the chord notes."},
  {"follower", "attack", "How fast the level rises to follow a louder sound, in ms."},
  {"follower", "release", "How fast it falls after the sound gets quieter, in ms."},
  {"follower", "floor", "The level in dB that counts as 0 on the output. Quieter sounds read 0."},
  {"follower", "gain", "dB added before the window, to push a quiet signal up to the range."},
  {"follower", "mode", "0 peak (follows the loudest sample), 1 rms (average power, closer to what you hear)."},
  {"follower", "invert", "1 flips the output so a louder sound gives a lower value: use it to duck a level."},
  {"sidechain", "level", "How loud the other instrument's sound comes in."},
  {"listen", "on", "1 plays the notes of the other instrument here too, 0 does not. A note that is already sounding still ends, so nothing sticks."},
  {"listen", "transpose", "Semitones added to every note heard, so another instrument can play them higher or lower."},
  {"listen", "level", "Scales the velocity of the notes heard: 1 as they are, 0.5 half as hard."},
  {"hall", "size", "Scales the room: larger is a bigger, slower-building space."},
  {"hall", "decay", "Time in seconds for the tail to fall by 60 dB."},
  {"hall", "damp", "How fast the highs die away in the tail. Higher is darker and softer."},
  {"hall", "predelay", "Delay in ms before the reverb starts. Keeps the dry sound clear of the tail."},
  {"hall", "diffusion", "How smeared the early reflections are. More is smoother; less is more echoey."},
  {"hall", "mod", "Slow movement of the delay lines to hide metallic ringing. Too much sounds seasick."},
  {"hall", "width", "Stereo width of the reverb. 0 is mono."},
  {"hall", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. 1 is all reverb."},
  {"plate", "predelay", "Delay in ms before the reverb starts."},
  {"plate", "bandwidth", "Brightness of what goes into the plate. Lower is darker and softer."},
  {"plate", "decay", "Time in seconds for the tail to fall by 60 dB."},
  {"plate", "damp", "How fast the highs die away in the tail."},
  {"plate", "diffusion", "Density of the plate. High is smooth and instant; low is more grainy."},
  {"plate", "mod", "Slow movement inside the plate to avoid metallic ringing."},
  {"plate", "width", "Stereo width of the reverb. 0 is mono."},
  {"plate", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. 1 is all reverb."},
  {"shimmer", "size", "Scales the room."},
  {"shimmer", "decay", "Time in seconds for the tail to fall by 60 dB. Longer tails climb higher."},
  {"shimmer", "damp", "How fast the highs die away in the tail."},
  {"shimmer", "predelay", "Delay in ms before the reverb starts."},
  {"shimmer", "pitch", "The interval the tail is shifted by each time round, in semitones. 12 is an octave up; negative goes down."},
  {"shimmer", "shimmer", "How much of the shifted tail comes back in. More climbs further and lasts longer; near 1 it keeps building."},
  {"shimmer", "tone", "Brightness of the shifted sound that is fed back. Lower is softer."},
  {"shimmer", "width", "Stereo width of the reverb. 0 is mono."},
  {"shimmer", "mix", "Dry/wet balance. 1 is all reverb."},
  {"subsynth", "wave1", "Oscillator 1 waveform: 0 saw, 1 square, 2 triangle, 3 sine."},
  {"subsynth", "wave2", "Oscillator 2 waveform: 0 saw, 1 square, 2 triangle, 3 sine."},
  {"subsynth", "pw", "Pulse width of the square waves. 0.5 is a symmetric square; away from it is thinner and more nasal."},
  {"subsynth", "osc2", "Level of oscillator 2 (oscillator 1 is always at full level)."},
  {"subsynth", "detune", "Detunes oscillator 2 against oscillator 1, in cents. A few cents gives a slow, fat beating."},
  {"subsynth", "tune2", "Transposes oscillator 2 against oscillator 1, in semitones."},
  {"subsynth", "sub", "Level of a sine one octave below, for weight."},
  {"subsynth", "noise", "Level of white noise mixed in."},
  {"subsynth", "unison", "Number of copies of each oscillator (1 to 7), spread in pitch and across the stereo field. More is a thicker supersaw sound."},
  {"subsynth", "spread", "Detune in cents between the outermost unison copies."},
  {"subsynth", "width", "How wide the unison copies are spread in stereo."},
  {"subsynth", "cutoff", "Cutoff of the 24 dB lowpass in Hz. The filter envelope and the `cut` input move it from here."},
  {"subsynth", "res", "Resonance of the filter. High values ring and, at 1, sing by themselves."},
  {"subsynth", "drive", "Saturation at the filter input. More bites and thickens."},
  {"subsynth", "fenv", "How many octaves the filter envelope adds to the cutoff at its peak (negative closes the filter)."},
  {"subsynth", "keytrack", "How much the cutoff follows the note. 1 follows it exactly, 0 ignores pitch."},
  {"subsynth", "fatk", "Filter envelope attack in ms: how fast the cutoff rises after a note starts."},
  {"subsynth", "fdec", "Filter envelope decay in ms: how long the cutoff takes to fall to the filter sustain level."},
  {"subsynth", "fsus", "Filter envelope sustain level while the note is held, from 0 to 1."},
  {"subsynth", "frel", "Filter envelope release in ms: how long the cutoff takes to close after the note ends."},
  {"subsynth", "atk", "Attack time in ms: how long the sound takes to rise to full level after a note starts."},
  {"subsynth", "dec", "Decay time in ms: how long it takes to fall from full level to the sustain level."},
  {"subsynth", "sus", "Sustain level held while the note is held, from 0 to 1 of full level."},
  {"subsynth", "rel", "Release time in ms: how long the sound takes to fade out after the note ends."},
  {"subsynth", "glide", "Time in ms to slide from the previous note to the new one. 0 jumps straight there."},
  {"subsynth", "slide", "In ticks: a note that follows another in the same column glides to its pitch over this long, in a straight line. 0 is instant."},
  {"subsynth", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound. 0 ignores velocity; 1 makes a soft note much quieter and duller."},
  {"subsynth", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"fourpole", "wave1", "Oscillator 1 wave: sine, triangle, square, up saw, down saw, three sawoids, two noises."},
  {"fourpole", "wave2", "Oscillator 2 wave; \"off\" leaves oscillator 1 alone."},
  {"fourpole", "seed", "Seed for the two noise waves: the same seed always makes the same wave."},
  {"fourpole", "phase", "Phase of oscillator 2 against oscillator 1, in cycles."},
  {"fourpole", "transpose", "Oscillator 2 against 1, in semitones."},
  {"fourpole", "detune", "Oscillator 2 against 1, in cents."},
  {"fourpole", "mix", "How the oscillators combine: 1+2, 1-2, 2-1, 1*2, 1/2, 2/1."},
  {"fourpole", "pstart", "Start of the part of the wave table one cycle plays (both oscillators)."},
  {"fourpole", "pend", "End of that part. A narrow window gives a thinner, harsher wave."},
  {"fourpole", "atk", "Envelope 1 (the amplitude envelope) attack, ms."},
  {"fourpole", "dec", "Envelope 1 decay, ms."},
  {"fourpole", "sus", "Envelope 1 sustain level."},
  {"fourpole", "rel", "Envelope 1 release, ms."},
  {"fourpole", "atk2", "Envelope 2 (a modulation envelope) attack, ms."},
  {"fourpole", "dec2", "Envelope 2 decay, ms."},
  {"fourpole", "sus2", "Envelope 2 sustain level."},
  {"fourpole", "rel2", "Envelope 2 release, ms."},
  {"fourpole", "f1type", "Filter 1: off, lowpass or highpass."},
  {"fourpole", "f1cut", "Filter 1 cutoff, 0 to 1000 (tenths of a percent of the Cutoff Limit)."},
  {"fourpole", "f1res", "Filter 1 resonance, 50 to 1000 (a Q of res/50)."},
  {"fourpole", "f2type", "Filter 2: off, slave (copy filter 1), lowpass or highpass."},
  {"fourpole", "f2cut", "Filter 2 cutoff, 1001 follows filter 1."},
  {"fourpole", "f2res", "Filter 2 resonance, 49 follows filter 1."},
  {"fourpole", "f3type", "Filter 3: off, slave (copy filter 1), lowpass or highpass."},
  {"fourpole", "f3cut", "Filter 3 cutoff, 1001 follows filter 1."},
  {"fourpole", "f3res", "Filter 3 resonance, 49 follows filter 1."},
  {"fourpole", "f4type", "Filter 4: off, slave (copy filter 1), lowpass or highpass."},
  {"fourpole", "f4cut", "Filter 4 cutoff, 1001 follows filter 1."},
  {"fourpole", "f4res", "Filter 4 resonance, 49 follows filter 1."},
  {"fourpole", "route", "How the four filters connect. A + sums filters that run side by side; > feeds the next stage."},
  {"fourpole", "limit", "What a cutoff of 100% means: a fixed frequency, or a multiple or fraction of the note being played."},
  {"fourpole", "inertia", "How slowly the filters follow a change of cutoff or resonance, ms."},
  {"fourpole", "glide", "Time to slide from the last note to the new one, ms."},
  {"fourpole", "vibdepth", "Vibrato depth. Cubic, so small values are subtle; negative inverts it."},
  {"fourpole", "vibrate", "Vibrato rate, Hz."},
  {"fourpole", "e1vr", "How much envelope 1 changes the vibrato rate."},
  {"fourpole", "e1vd", "How much envelope 1 changes the vibrato depth."},
  {"fourpole", "e1cut", "How much envelope 1 changes the filter cutoffs (cutoff x (1 + amount x envelope))."},
  {"fourpole", "e1res", "How much envelope 1 changes the resonance."},
  {"fourpole", "e1pit", "How much envelope 1 changes the pitch (1 is an octave at full level)."},
  {"fourpole", "e2vr", "How much envelope 2 changes the vibrato rate."},
  {"fourpole", "e2vd", "How much envelope 2 changes the vibrato depth."},
  {"fourpole", "e2cut", "How much envelope 2 changes the filter cutoffs."},
  {"fourpole", "e2res", "How much envelope 2 changes the resonance."},
  {"fourpole", "e2pit", "How much envelope 2 changes the pitch."},
  {"fourpole", "e2amp", "How much envelope 2 changes the loudness, on top of envelope 1."},
  {"fourpole", "tuning", "Pitch of A, Hz."},
  {"fourpole", "detail", "Samples between modulation updates. Higher saves CPU, lower is smoother."},
  {"fourpole", "vel", "How much note velocity changes the level."},
  {"fourpole", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"oberheim", "wave1", "Oscillator 1 waveform: 0 saw, 1 pulse, 2 triangle."},
  {"oberheim", "wave2", "Oscillator 2 waveform: 0 saw, 1 pulse, 2 triangle."},
  {"oberheim", "pw", "Pulse width of the pulse waves. 0.5 is a symmetric square."},
  {"oberheim", "pwm", "How far a slow LFO swings the pulse width. Gives the moving, chorused pulse sound."},
  {"oberheim", "pwmrate", "Speed of the pulse-width LFO in Hz."},
  {"oberheim", "osc2", "Level of oscillator 2."},
  {"oberheim", "detune", "Detunes oscillator 2 against oscillator 1, in cents."},
  {"oberheim", "tune2", "Transposes oscillator 2 against oscillator 1, in semitones."},
  {"oberheim", "sync", "1 restarts oscillator 2 every time oscillator 1 completes a cycle: the classic hard-sync sound. Sweep oscillator 2's pitch (tune2 or sweep) to hear it."},
  {"oberheim", "sweep", "Semitones the filter envelope adds to oscillator 2's pitch. With sync on it gives the sweeping sync lead sound."},
  {"oberheim", "noise", "Level of white noise mixed in."},
  {"oberheim", "cutoff", "Cutoff of the SEM-style 12 dB filter in Hz. The filter envelope and the `cut` input move it from here."},
  {"oberheim", "res", "Resonance of the filter. At 1 it self-oscillates."},
  {"oberheim", "mode", "Sweeps the filter from lowpass (0) through notch (0.5) to highpass (1)."},
  {"oberheim", "bp", "Blends the output towards bandpass."},
  {"oberheim", "drive", "Saturation at the filter input. More bites and thickens."},
  {"oberheim", "fenv", "How many octaves the filter envelope adds to the cutoff at its peak (negative closes the filter)."},
  {"oberheim", "keytrack", "How much the cutoff follows the note. 1 follows it exactly, 0 ignores pitch."},
  {"oberheim", "fatk", "Filter envelope attack in ms: how fast the cutoff rises after a note starts."},
  {"oberheim", "fdec", "Filter envelope decay in ms: how long the cutoff takes to fall to the filter sustain level."},
  {"oberheim", "fsus", "Filter envelope sustain level while the note is held, from 0 to 1."},
  {"oberheim", "frel", "Filter envelope release in ms: how long the cutoff takes to close after the note ends."},
  {"oberheim", "atk", "Attack time in ms: how long the sound takes to rise to full level after a note starts."},
  {"oberheim", "dec", "Decay time in ms: how long it takes to fall from full level to the sustain level."},
  {"oberheim", "sus", "Sustain level held while the note is held, from 0 to 1 of full level."},
  {"oberheim", "rel", "Release time in ms: how long the sound takes to fade out after the note ends."},
  {"oberheim", "glide", "Time in ms to slide from the previous note to the new one. 0 jumps straight there."},
  {"oberheim", "vib", "Depth of the built-in vibrato, in cents."},
  {"oberheim", "vrate", "Speed of the vibrato in Hz."},
  {"oberheim", "drift", "Slow random wander of each voice's pitch and cutoff, like an analog synth. Each voice drifts differently."},
  {"oberheim", "scatter", "Each note lands at a random place in the stereo field, up to this far from the centre."},
  {"oberheim", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound. 0 ignores velocity; 1 makes a soft note much quieter and duller."},
  {"oberheim", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"piano", "decay", "How long the slow, ringing part of the sound takes to fall by 60 dB, in seconds, at middle C. Bass notes ring longer than this, treble notes shorter."},
  {"piano", "prompt", "How much of the quick-fading \"prompt\" sound there is next to the slow \"after\" sound. A piano has both: one string of each note dies fast, the others ring on. 0 is all after sound."},
  {"piano", "hard", "How hard the felt of the hammer is. Soft felt is mellow; hard felt is bright and percussive."},
  {"piano", "pos", "Where along the string the hammer strikes, as a fraction of its length. Around 12% is a real piano; towards 50% it gets hollow, near the end thin."},
  {"piano", "bright", "How slowly the high partials die away. Low is a dull, short sound; high is a bright, ringing one."},
  {"piano", "inharm", "Stiffness of the strings: how far the high partials run sharp of exact multiples of the pitch. This is what makes a piano sound like a piano and not an organ. 0 is perfectly harmonic."},
  {"piano", "detune", "The two or three strings of a note are tuned a little apart, in cents. It makes the slow beating of a real piano and widens the sound."},
  {"piano", "body", "How much of the wooden body's resonances (in the low register) is mixed in."},
  {"piano", "thump", "The soft thud of the hammer and key hitting their rests, heard at the start of each note."},
  {"piano", "width", "How far notes are spread across the stereo field by pitch, bass to the left and treble to the right. 0 is mono."},
  {"piano", "pedal", "The sustain pedal: 0.5 or more is down. Released notes keep ringing while it is down, and are damped when it comes up. Expose it as a pattern column to play it."},
  {"piano", "release", "How fast the damper stills a string after the key is let go (when the pedal is up), in ms."},
  {"piano", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones (12 is an octave)."},
  {"piano", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound. 0 ignores velocity; 1 makes a soft note much quieter and duller."},
  {"piano", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"addsynth", "harmonics", "How many partials are used (1 to 32)."},
  {"addsynth", "tilt", "Brightens (+) or darkens (-) the sound by adding dB per octave across the partials."},
  {"addsynth", "evenodd", "-1 silences the even partials (hollow, square-like); +1 silences the odd ones."},
  {"addsynth", "stretch", "Spreads the partials away from exact multiples of the pitch. 0 is harmonic; higher is bell or piano-like."},
  {"addsynth", "drift", "Gives every partial a fixed random detune, so the sound shimmers instead of buzzing."},
  {"addsynth", "hdecay", "Makes higher partials die faster, per second per octave above the fundamental."},
  {"addsynth", "width", "Odd partials lean left and even ones right, for stereo width."},
  {"addsynth", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones (12 is an octave)."},
  {"addsynth", "glide", "Time in ms to slide from the previous note to the new one. 0 jumps straight there."},
  {"addsynth", "atk", "Attack time in ms: how long the sound takes to rise to full level after a note starts."},
  {"addsynth", "dec", "Decay time in ms: how long it takes to fall from full level to the sustain level."},
  {"addsynth", "sus", "Sustain level held while the note is held, from 0 to 1 of full level."},
  {"addsynth", "rel", "Release time in ms: how long the sound takes to fade out after the note ends."},
  {"addsynth", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound. 0 ignores velocity; 1 makes a soft note much quieter and duller."},
  {"addsynth", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"fmsynth", "algo", "Which operators modulate which, and which you hear (the carriers). The names show the routing: 4>3>2>1 is a chain into operator 1."},
  {"fmsynth", "index", "Modulation depth: how strongly the modulators bend the operators they feed. More is brighter and more metallic."},
  {"fmsynth", "fb", "Operator 4 modulating itself: from a bright edge to noise."},
  {"fmsynth", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones (12 is an octave)."},
  {"fmsynth", "glide", "Time in ms to slide from the previous note to the new one. 0 jumps straight there."},
  {"fmsynth", "vel", "How much velocity scales the levels, and so the brightness."},
  {"fmsynth", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"fmsynth", "ratio1", "Operator 1 frequency as a multiple of the note. 1 is the pitch itself; whole numbers are harmonic, others are bell-like."},
  {"fmsynth", "fine1", "Operator 1 fine tune in cents."},
  {"fmsynth", "level1", "Operator 1 level. For a modulator it sets how much it modulates."},
  {"fmsynth", "atk1", "Operator 1 attack in ms. For a modulator, a slow attack brings the brightness in gradually."},
  {"fmsynth", "dec1", "Operator 1 decay in ms, down to its sustain level."},
  {"fmsynth", "sus1", "Operator 1 sustain level while the note is held, 0 to 1. For a modulator it is how bright the held note stays."},
  {"fmsynth", "rel1", "Operator 1 release in ms."},
  {"fmsynth", "ratio2", "Operator 2 frequency as a multiple of the note."},
  {"fmsynth", "fine2", "Operator 2 fine tune in cents."},
  {"fmsynth", "level2", "Operator 2 level. For a modulator it sets how much it modulates."},
  {"fmsynth", "atk2", "Operator 2 attack in ms. For a modulator, a slow attack brings the brightness in gradually."},
  {"fmsynth", "dec2", "Operator 2 decay in ms, down to its sustain level."},
  {"fmsynth", "sus2", "Operator 2 sustain level while the note is held, 0 to 1. For a modulator it is how bright the held note stays."},
  {"fmsynth", "rel2", "Operator 2 release in ms."},
  {"fmsynth", "ratio3", "Operator 3 frequency as a multiple of the note."},
  {"fmsynth", "fine3", "Operator 3 fine tune in cents."},
  {"fmsynth", "level3", "Operator 3 level. For a modulator it sets how much it modulates."},
  {"fmsynth", "atk3", "Operator 3 attack in ms. For a modulator, a slow attack brings the brightness in gradually."},
  {"fmsynth", "dec3", "Operator 3 decay in ms, down to its sustain level."},
  {"fmsynth", "sus3", "Operator 3 sustain level while the note is held, 0 to 1. For a modulator it is how bright the held note stays."},
  {"fmsynth", "rel3", "Operator 3 release in ms."},
  {"fmsynth", "ratio4", "Operator 4 frequency as a multiple of the note."},
  {"fmsynth", "fine4", "Operator 4 fine tune in cents."},
  {"fmsynth", "level4", "Operator 4 level. For a modulator it sets how much it modulates."},
  {"fmsynth", "atk4", "Operator 4 attack in ms. For a modulator, a slow attack brings the brightness in gradually."},
  {"fmsynth", "dec4", "Operator 4 decay in ms, down to its sustain level."},
  {"fmsynth", "sus4", "Operator 4 sustain level while the note is held, 0 to 1. For a modulator it is how bright the held note stays."},
  {"fmsynth", "rel4", "Operator 4 release in ms."},
  {"dvii", "voice", "Which of the 32 voices in the bank to play (0 is the first). A pattern command can change it, so the sound changes on a row."},
  {"dvii", "tune", "Transposes the voice, in semitones (fractions detune)."},
  {"dvii", "vel", "How much velocity counts. 0 ignores it, 1 is the voice's own velocity sensitivity."},
  {"dvii", "level", "Output level."},
  {"dvii", "lfo", "Scales the voice's LFO depth, for pitch and amplitude together. 0 turns the vibrato and tremolo off."},
  {"dvii", "speed", "Scales every envelope rate: above 1 is faster, below 1 slower."},
  {"dvii", "gritty", "On: operator outputs are rounded to a coarse step, a little of the noise of the old converters."},
  {"dvii", "mute", "Silences operators: bit 1 is operator 1, bit 2 operator 2, and so on (the M buttons in the panel set it)."},
  {"dvii", "solo", "When any bit is set, only those operators sound (the S buttons in the panel set it)."},
  {"wavetable", "source", "0 uses the stack of wave frames (table); 1 uses the pair of waves you cut from a sample."},
  {"wavetable", "position", "Where in the table to read, 0 to 1. It blends the two frames it falls between."},
  {"wavetable", "mix", "In pair mode: blends from wave A (0) to wave B (1)."},
  {"wavetable", "snap", "1 jumps from frame to frame instead of blending."},
  {"wavetable", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones (12 is an octave)."},
  {"wavetable", "fine", "Fine tuning in cents (100 cents is a semitone)."},
  {"wavetable", "glide", "Time in ms to slide from the previous note to the new one. 0 jumps straight there."},
  {"wavetable", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound. 0 ignores velocity; 1 makes a soft note much quieter and duller."},
  {"wavetable", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"wavetable", "attack", "Attack time in ms: how long the sound takes to rise to full level after a note starts."},
  {"wavetable", "decay", "Decay time in ms: how long it takes to fall from full level to the sustain level (to fall by 60 dB when the shape is exponential)."},
  {"wavetable", "sustain", "Sustain level held while the note is held, from 0 to 1 of full level. At 0 the sound dies away by itself after the decay."},
  {"wavetable", "release", "Release time in ms: how long the sound takes to fade out after the note ends."},
  {"wavetable", "pamt", "How far the second envelope moves the position (or the mix) over a note. Negative moves the other way."},
  {"wavetable", "patk", "Attack of the position envelope, in ms."},
  {"wavetable", "pdec", "Decay of the position envelope, in ms."},
  {"wavetable", "psus", "Sustain level of the position envelope, 0 to 1."},
  {"wavetable", "prel", "Release of the position envelope, in ms."},
  {"wavetable", "lfoRate", "Speed in Hz of the LFO that wobbles the position."},
  {"wavetable", "lfoDepth", "How far the LFO wobbles the position (or mix)."},
  {"wavetable", "smooth", "How fast the position (or mix) may move when it is changed, in ms. More removes the clicks of a jump; 0 follows instantly."},
  {"granular", "sample", "Which sample of this instrument's list to use (0 is the first)."},
  {"granular", "position", "Where in the sample the cloud starts when a note starts, from 0 (start) to 1 (end). Scan then moves it on from there; spray scatters the grains around it."},
  {"granular", "spray", "Random scatter of the start positions. Higher is a cloudier, less focused sound."},
  {"granular", "scan", "Moves the position along the sample over time, as a fraction of it per second. Negative goes backwards."},
  {"granular", "size", "Length of each grain in ms. Short grains are buzzy and pitched; long ones keep the source recognisable."},
  {"granular", "density", "How many grains start every second. More is thicker and smoother."},
  {"granular", "shape", "How much of each grain is fade in and out. 1 is a smooth bell; near 0 is a hard-edged slice."},
  {"granular", "tune", "Transposes the grains in semitones."},
  {"granular", "pitchRand", "Random pitch scatter of each grain, in cents."},
  {"granular", "keytrack", "1: grain pitch follows the note played. 0: the pitch is fixed."},
  {"granular", "root", "The note at which the sample plays at its own pitch."},
  {"granular", "reverse", "The chance (0 to 1) that a grain plays backwards."},
  {"granular", "spread", "How far grains are scattered across the stereo field."},
  {"granular", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound. 0 ignores velocity; 1 makes a soft note much quieter and duller."},
  {"granular", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"granular", "attack", "Attack in ms of the whole cloud after a note starts."},
  {"granular", "decay", "Decay in ms of the cloud to the sustain level."},
  {"granular", "sustain", "Sustain level of the cloud while the note is held."},
  {"granular", "release", "Release in ms of the cloud after the note ends."},
  {"woodblock", "tone", "Frequency in Hz of the lowest mode at the root note."},
  {"woodblock", "keytrack", "1: the pitch follows the note played. 0: the pitch is fixed."},
  {"woodblock", "root", "The note at which the block plays at its own tone."},
  {"woodblock", "inharm", "Spreads the modes away from their natural ratios: higher is more clangy."},
  {"woodblock", "hardness", "The stick. Hard excites the high modes (bright, clicky); soft only the low ones (dull). Note velocity adds to it."},
  {"woodblock", "damping", "Time in ms for the lowest mode to fall by 60 dB."},
  {"woodblock", "loss", "How much faster the higher modes lose their energy than the lowest one. High: a click that goes to a knock."},
  {"woodblock", "bend", "How many semitones the pitch envelope raises the start of the sound; it then falls back."},
  {"woodblock", "vel", "How much the note velocity changes the level (and the brightness)."},
  {"woodblock", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"woodblock", "attack", "Volume envelope attack in ms."},
  {"woodblock", "decay", "Volume envelope decay in ms."},
  {"woodblock", "sustain", "Volume envelope sustain while the note is held. 0 for a plain knock."},
  {"woodblock", "release", "Volume envelope release in ms."},
  {"woodblock", "patk", "Pitch envelope attack in ms."},
  {"woodblock", "pdec", "Pitch envelope decay in ms: how fast the pitch falls back."},
  {"woodblock", "psus", "Pitch envelope sustain, as a part of the bend."},
  {"woodblock", "prel", "Pitch envelope release in ms."},
  {"jawharp", "vowel", "The shape of the mouth, from u (0) through o, a and e to i (1). It moves the resonances that pick out the harmonics of the reed: sweep it for the wah and the tune."},
  {"jawharp", "mouth", "The size of the cavity in semitones. Negative is a bigger mouth (lower, hollower), positive a smaller one."},
  {"jawharp", "track", "How far the vowel follows the note played. 0 leaves the mouth where it is, so every note has the same colour; 1 moves it a semitone for each semitone of the note."},
  {"jawharp", "focus", "How sharply the mouth picks harmonics (the resonators' Q). Low is a soft vowel, high is a ringing, whistling overtone."},
  {"jawharp", "drive", "How narrow the puff of air is each time the reed swings through the gap. Narrow means many harmonics for the mouth to choose from."},
  {"jawharp", "twang", "The pluck: the reed starts a little sharp and slaps. 0 is a smooth start."},
  {"jawharp", "breath", "Noise in the air flow."},
  {"jawharp", "body", "How much of the plain, unresonated reed comes through next to the mouth."},
  {"jawharp", "decay", "Time in seconds for the reed to fall by 60 dB."},
  {"jawharp", "tune", "Tuning in semitones."},
  {"jawharp", "vel", "How much the note velocity changes the level."},
  {"jawharp", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"jawharp", "release", "How fast the reed is damped, in ms, after the note ends."},
  {"snarl", "vco1wave", "Waveform of oscillator 1: saw, triangle, pulse or noise."},
  {"snarl", "vco1oct", "Octave of oscillator 1: 32', 16', 8' or 4'."},
  {"snarl", "vco1pw", "Pulse width of oscillator 1 (for the pulse wave): higher is thinner."},
  {"snarl", "vco2wave", "Waveform of oscillator 2: saw, square, pulse, or ring, which multiplies it with oscillator 1 for bells and clangs."},
  {"snarl", "vco2oct", "Octave of oscillator 2."},
  {"snarl", "vco2pw", "Pulse width of oscillator 2 (for the pulse wave)."},
  {"snarl", "vco2pitch", "Pitch of oscillator 2 against oscillator 1, in semitones. A fifth (7) or an octave (12) fills the sound out."},
  {"snarl", "detune", "A little extra pitch for oscillator 2, in cents: slow beating against oscillator 1."},
  {"snarl", "mix1", "Level of oscillator 1 into the filters."},
  {"snarl", "mix2", "Level of oscillator 2 into the filters."},
  {"snarl", "noise", "Level of the noise generator into the filters."},
  {"snarl", "hpcut", "Cutoff in Hz of the high-pass filter, which comes first. Raised, it thins the bass out of the sound."},
  {"snarl", "hppeak", "Resonance of the high-pass filter. Up high it sings at its cutoff."},
  {"snarl", "lpcut", "Cutoff in Hz of the low-pass filter, which comes second."},
  {"snarl", "lppeak", "Resonance of the low-pass filter. With both peaks up and the two cutoffs close, the filters scream."},
  {"snarl", "edge", "How hard the second filter is driven: low is clean, high bites and growls."},
  {"snarl", "eg1delay", "Envelope 1: time in ms before it starts to rise."},
  {"snarl", "eg1attack", "Envelope 1: time in ms to rise to the top. It stays there while the key is down."},
  {"snarl", "eg1release", "Envelope 1: time in ms to fall after the key is let go."},
  {"snarl", "eg1pitch", "How far envelope 1 raises (or, negative, drops) the pitch of both oscillators."},
  {"snarl", "eg1hp", "How far envelope 1 opens (or closes) the high-pass filter."},
  {"snarl", "eg1lp", "How far envelope 1 opens (or closes) the low-pass filter."},
  {"snarl", "attack", "Amplifier envelope: attack in ms."},
  {"snarl", "hold", "Amplifier envelope: how long in ms it stays at the top before the decay."},
  {"snarl", "decay", "Amplifier envelope: time in ms to fall to the sustain level."},
  {"snarl", "sustain", "Amplifier envelope: the level held while the key is down."},
  {"snarl", "release", "Amplifier envelope: release in ms."},
  {"snarl", "mgwave", "Wave of the modulation generator: triangle, saw, ramp, square or random."},
  {"snarl", "mgrate", "Speed of the modulation generator, in Hz."},
  {"snarl", "mgpitch", "How much the modulation generator moves the pitch (vibrato)."},
  {"snarl", "mghp", "How much it moves the high-pass cutoff."},
  {"snarl", "mglp", "How much it moves the low-pass cutoff (wah)."},
  {"snarl", "mgamp", "How much it moves the level (tremolo)."},
  {"snarl", "port", "Glide time in ms between notes that overlap. 0 is off."},
  {"snarl", "keytrack", "How much both cutoffs follow the note played."},
  {"snarl", "vel", "How much the note velocity changes the level."},
  {"snarl", "tune", "Tuning in semitones."},
  {"snarl", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"specsynth", "sample", "Which sample of this instrument's list to use (0 is the first)."},
  {"specsynth", "position", "Where in the sample the spectrum is taken from, 0 to 1."},
  {"specsynth", "scan", "Moves the spectrum position along the sample over time, so the timbre evolves. Negative goes back."},
  {"specsynth", "blur", "Smooths the spectrum across neighbouring frames and partials. Higher is softer."},
  {"specsynth", "contrast", "Sharpens the spectrum: loud partials get louder and quiet ones quieter."},
  {"specsynth", "partials", "How many partials are played (up to 64). More is richer and costs more CPU."},
  {"specsynth", "root", "The note at which the sample's own harmonics line up with the pitch you play."},
  {"specsynth", "tilt", "Brightens (+) or darkens (-) the sound, in dB per octave."},
  {"specsynth", "evenodd", "-1 silences the even partials, +1 silences the odd ones."},
  {"specsynth", "stretch", "Spreads the partials away from exact multiples of the pitch. 0 is harmonic."},
  {"specsynth", "width", "Stereo width. 0 is mono, 1 is fully spread."},
  {"specsynth", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones (12 is an octave)."},
  {"specsynth", "glide", "Time in ms to slide from the previous note to the new one. 0 jumps straight there."},
  {"specsynth", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound. 0 ignores velocity; 1 makes a soft note much quieter and duller."},
  {"specsynth", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"specsynth", "attack", "Attack in ms."},
  {"specsynth", "decay", "Decay in ms to the sustain level."},
  {"specsynth", "sustain", "Sustain level while the note is held."},
  {"specsynth", "release", "Release in ms."},
  {"string", "decay", "Time in seconds for the string to fall by 60 dB. It holds for every pitch."},
  {"string", "damp", "How fast the highs die away compared to the lows, as on a real string. Higher is duller sooner."},
  {"string", "bright", "How bright the strike is: a soft finger (low) to a hard pick (high)."},
  {"string", "pos", "Where along the string it is plucked. 0.5 is the middle, which loses the even harmonics; near the end it is thin and nasal."},
  {"string", "tune", "Transposes the pitch, in semitones (12 is an octave)."},
  {"string", "vel", "How much the velocity of each note scales the sound. 0 ignores velocity; 1 makes a soft note much quieter and duller."},
  {"string", "level", "Output level of this node."},
  {"string", "release", "How fast the string is damped, in ms, after the note ends."},
  {"hat808", "tune", "Moves the whole metal bank up or down, in semitones."},
  {"hat808", "tone", "High-pass frequency in Hz. Higher is thinner and more sizzly."},
  {"hat808", "snap", "A short bright click at the start of each hit, like the stick."},
  {"hat808", "level", "Overall level of the hat."},
  {"hat808", "cdec", "How long the closed hat takes to fall by 40 dB, in ms."},
  {"hat808", "odec", "How long the open hat takes to fall by 40 dB, in ms."},
  {"hat808", "clvl", "Level of the closed hat against the open one."},
  {"hat808", "olvl", "Level of the open hat against the closed one."},
  {"hat808", "choke", "1: a closed hit cuts the open hat with a quick 8 ms fade. 0: the open hat rings on."},
  {"hat2", "tune", "Moves the whole metal bank up or down, in semitones."},
  {"hat2", "spread", "Pulls the six metal tones off their exact ratios: more is a less pure, more clangy ring."},
  {"hat2", "noise", "Mix of white noise against the metal: 0 is all metal, 1 is all noise."},
  {"hat2", "body", "Centre of the band-pass in Hz: where the hat is brightest."},
  {"hat2", "tone", "High-pass in Hz. Higher is thinner and more sizzly."},
  {"hat2", "human", "Every hit a little different: its level, brightness, the pitch of the metal and where its waves start."},
  {"hat2", "level", "Overall level of the hat."},
  {"hat2", "crate", "How fast the closed hat's volume drawing plays. 1 takes 140 ms, 2 takes half as long."},
  {"hat2", "orate", "How fast the open hat's volume drawing plays. 1 takes 700 ms."},
  {"hat2", "clvl", "Level of the closed hat against the open one."},
  {"hat2", "olvl", "Level of the open hat against the closed one."},
  {"hat2", "choke", "1: a closed hit cuts the open hat. 0: the open hat rings on."},
  {"hat2", "flange", "How much of the flanger is heard, after the hat and before the crusher."},
  {"hat2", "frate", "Speed of the flanger sweep in Hz."},
  {"hat2", "fdepth", "How far the flanger sweeps."},
  {"hat2", "ffeed", "Flanger feedback. Negative values make a hollower sound."},
  {"hat2", "crush", "How much of the bit crusher is heard."},
  {"hat2", "bits", "Bit depth of the crusher. Lower is rougher."},
  {"hat2", "down", "The crusher holds each sample for this many, which lowers the sample rate and adds ringing alias tones."},
  {"bongo", "strike", "How the head is hit. 0 is muted (fingers left on the skin), 0.5 an open tone, 1 a slap across the rim. Points between mix the two nearest."},
  {"bongo", "ring", "How long an open tone rings, in ms. Muted hits ring for a fifth of this, slaps for half."},
  {"bongo", "skin", "How much faster the high modes die than the low ones: higher is a duller, wetter head."},
  {"bongo", "snap", "The pitch jump of a hard hit, as the skin tightens and falls back in a few milliseconds."},
  {"bongo", "crack", "The noise of the hand on the skin. It follows the strike: slaps crack, muted hits barely do."},
  {"bongo", "shell", "The wooden shell's knock, a short note an octave under the head."},
  {"bongo", "rim", "The high ring from the hoop."},
  {"bongo", "human", "Every hit a little different: its pitch, level and brightness."},
  {"bongo", "tune", "Moves the pitch in semitones. A high bongo is around D5, a low one around A4."},
  {"bongo", "vel", "How much the velocity changes level and brightness."},
  {"bongo", "level", "Overall level."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "low", "The bottom of the band that is arrayed, in Hz. Drag the left end of the pink strip on the panel."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "high", "The top of the band, in Hz. Drag the right end of the strip, or drag the strip to move the whole band."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "mode", "Mirror reads every second copy backwards, so the seams join and the intervals turn upside down inside it. Repeat plays every copy the same way. Overtone makes no tiles: every tone in the band is copied to its overtones (x2, x3, x4 ... of its own frequency) and undertones (x1/2, x1/3 ...), so the copies bunch together going up like the partials of a note."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "rotate", "Slides the pattern along the range, in band widths. A slow sweep turns the kaleidoscope."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "copies", "0 fills the whole range with copies. A number keeps the source and that many copies either side (mirror and repeat modes; the overtone mode has its own counts)."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "edge", "How far in semitones the copies fade at their seams where the ends do not match (repeat mode, and the last copy)."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "level", "Level of the arrayed sound, in dB."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "mix", "Dry sound against the arrayed sound."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "size", "Frame length: 1024, 2048 or 4096 samples. Longer is cleaner in the low range and arrives later."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "stretch", "Overtone mode: pulls the multiples off the whole numbers, upward, like a stiff string or a bell. 0 is an exact overtone series; high is a clanging, inharmonic one."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "decay", "Overtone mode: how fast the higher copies get quieter. 0 keeps them all as loud as the source, 0.5 is 1/n like a plain overtone series, 1 fades faster."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "overtones", "Overtone mode: how many copies are made above the source: x2, x3, x4 ... up to this many. The ones that would land past the top of the range are left out."},
  {"kaleidoscope", "under", "Overtone mode: how many copies are made below the source: x1/2, x1/3 ... 0 makes none."},
  {"reflect", "width", "Width of the room in metres."},
  {"reflect", "depth", "Length of the room in metres (the listener faces the far wall)."},
  {"reflect", "height", "Height of the room in metres. A low ceiling brings its echo close."},
  {"reflect", "srcx", "Where the sound is in the room, across. Drag the orange dot on the plan."},
  {"reflect", "srcy", "Where the sound is in the room, along. Drag the orange dot on the plan."},
  {"reflect", "lisx", "Where you listen from, across. Drag the blue dot on the plan."},
  {"reflect", "lisy", "Where you listen from, along. Drag the blue dot on the plan."},
  {"reflect", "absorb", "How much sound each wall takes when a reflection hits it. High is a padded room: the echoes die quickly."},
  {"reflect", "taps", "How many of the earliest reflections are heard (up to 32). More is denser and costs a little more."},
  {"reflect", "damp", "Low-pass on the reflections in Hz: walls take the top off the sound."},
  {"reflect", "mix", "Level of the reflections against the dry sound."},
  {"headpan", "angle", "Where the sound is, in degrees round your head: 0 in front, 90 on the right, 180 behind, -90 on the left. Drag the dot on the plan."},
  {"headpan", "distance", "How far away, in metres. Farther is quieter."},
  {"headpan", "head", "Width of the head in cm. A wider head makes a longer time difference between the ears."},
  {"headpan", "shadow", "How much the head dulls and quiets the far ear, and the ear dulls what comes from behind. 0 leaves only the time difference."},
  {"headpan", "mix", "Dry/wet balance."},
  {"haas", "time", "How late the delayed side is, in ms. Up to about 35 ms it is heard as width, not as an echo; longer it turns into a slapback."},
  {"haas", "gain", "Level of the delayed side."},
  {"haas", "side", "Which side is late: 0 left, 1 right. The sound seems to come from the other side."},
  {"haas", "damp", "Low-pass on the late side in Hz: a duller copy sounds more natural."},
  {"haas", "mix", "Dry/wet balance."},
  {"ringmod", "freq", "The carrier in Hz when it is not following the pitch (and when no pitch is heard)."},
  {"ringmod", "track", "On: the carrier follows the pitch of the sound, so sidebands stay in tune with the note."},
  {"ringmod", "ratio", "Carrier as a multiple of the pitch: 1 doubles the overtones, 2 gives octaves, 1.5 fifths, other values clang."},
  {"ringmod", "offset", "Hz added to the carrier. Moves the sidebands off the harmonics for a bell-like, inharmonic sound."},
  {"ringmod", "wave", "Shape of the carrier: sine, triangle, square or saw. Sharper waves add more sidebands."},
  {"ringmod", "depth", "How much the carrier multiplies the sound. 1 is a full ring modulator, less is a tremolo-like blend."},
  {"ringmod", "mix", "Dry/wet balance."},
  {"sympathy", "root", "The lowest string, as a MIDI note (48 is C3). Use the - and + on the panel to read it as a note."},
  {"sympathy", "chord", "Which notes are strung above the root: open (root and fifth), major, minor, maj7, min7, sus4, dim or whole tone. The strings climb through the octaves."},
  {"sympathy", "strings", "How many strings, up to 12. More fills in higher octaves of the chord."},
  {"sympathy", "ringtime", "How long a string rings after it is excited, in ms (time to fall by 60 dB)."},
  {"sympathy", "damp", "Low-pass inside each string in Hz: lower gives a warmer ring that loses its top as it fades."},
  {"sympathy", "detune", "How far the strings are off true pitch in cents. A little makes them shimmer, a lot makes them out of tune."},
  {"sympathy", "width", "How far from the middle the strings sit, by how far each is from the played note (the note itself stays central; octaves and neighbours move out). A little is piano-like, all the way is wide."},
  {"sympathy", "mix", "Level of the strings against the dry sound."},
  {"sympathy", "follow", "On: the strings are tuned to the notes the instrument plays, not to the chord. Each note gets the nearest piano strings to its octave, twelfth and so on, so what rings answers what is played. Root and chord are not used then."},
  {"sympathy", "partials", "Follow notes: how many harmonics of each note get a string: 1 only the note itself, 2 adds the octave, 3 the twelfth, 4 two octaves ... Newest notes and lowest harmonics are strung first, up to the number of strings."},
  {"notepan", "style", "Where each note is put before any rotation. keyboard: by pitch, low left and high right. fan: the held notes spread evenly, in the order played. alternate: left, right, left ... random: a fixed place for each pitch. cluster: by how far the note is from the average of the notes held. mirror: notes in pairs either side of the centre."},
  {"notepan", "range", "How much of the stereo field is used. 0 puts every note in the centre, 1 reaches hard left and right."},
  {"notepan", "centre", "Moves the whole field to the left (negative) or right (positive)."},
  {"notepan", "glide", "How long a note takes to slide to a new place when the notes held change, in ms (the fan and cluster styles move as notes come and go)."},
  {"notepan", "rate", "How fast the notes turn round you, in cycles per second (real time, not tied to the tempo). Negative turns the other way. 0 holds still."},
  {"notepan", "amp", "How far the swing goes: 1 is half a turn either way. Not used by the orbit shape."},
  {"notepan", "shape", "swing goes back and forth smoothly, triangle goes back and forth in straight sweeps, orbit goes all the way round, passing behind you."},
  {"notepan", "spread", "Starts each note's turn a little later than the last. 0 and all notes turn together; more and a chord opens and closes like a fan."},
  {"notepan", "behind", "How much a note that has gone round behind you gets quieter and duller. 0 leaves it as it was."},
  {"combperc", "decay", "How long the combs ring, in ms to fall by 60 dB. Short is a tick or knock, long is a bell or a pipe."},
  {"combperc", "damp", "How fast the highs die inside the ring compared to the lows. Higher is duller and more wooden."},
  {"combperc", "spread", "Where the four combs sit against each other. 0 is exact multiples of the pitch (a tuned tom or bell); 1 is inharmonic and metallic (a clang, pipe or industrial hit)."},
  {"combperc", "hollow", "1: the feedback is flipped, which drops the pitch an octave and leaves only the odd multiples. A hollow, wooden sound."},
  {"combperc", "click", "Length of the noise strike that excites the combs, in ms. Short is a sharp tick, long is a soft thump or a blown sound."},
  {"combperc", "bright", "How bright the strike is. Dull is a mallet, bright is a stick."},
  {"combperc", "tune", "Tunes the combs in semitones, on top of the note played."},
  {"combperc", "sweep", "How many octaves above the pitch the hit starts, falling to pitch over Sweep time. This is the techno tom zap."},
  {"combperc", "sdec", "How fast the sweep falls to pitch, in ms."},
  {"combperc", "drive", "Saturates the ring. A little thickens it, a lot makes it rough."},
  {"combperc", "vel", "How much velocity changes the strike. 0 plays every hit at the same strength."},
